.\" Manpage for pcloudc(1) - pCloud sync daemon control-plane CLI.
.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
.\" User-facing rewrite. Subcommands and flags are kept in sync with
.\"   crates/pcloud-cli/src/{app,commands,globals,exit_code,doctor,
.\"   field_selector,json_output,main}.rs
.\" and crates/pcloud-config/src/env.rs.
.\" Do not add commands, flags, or env vars that do not exist in source.
.\" This is pre-alpha software. Wording must not imply production readiness.
.TH PCLOUDC 1 "2026-04-16" "pcloud-rs Rust rewrite (pre-alpha)" "User Commands"
.\" =====================================================================
.SH NAME
pcloudc \- control-plane CLI for the pCloud sync daemon (pre-alpha)
.\"
.\" pcloudc is a thin client that speaks to the long-running daemon over a
.\" local IPC socket. It does not itself perform sync or mount work.
.\" =====================================================================
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcloudc
.RI [ GLOBAL-OPTIONS ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.PP
.B pcloudc
.B login
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR EMAIL ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-stdin ]
.RB [ \-\-save\-password ]
.PP
.B pcloudc
.B sync
.RB { add | list | remove | pause | resume | localscan }
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.PP
.B pcloudc
.B mount
.RI [ MOUNTPOINT ]
.PP
.B pcloudc
.RB [ \-\-json | \-\-output
.IR FORMAT ]
.RB [ \-q | \-\-quiet ]
.RB [ \-v | \-vv | \-vvv | \-\-verbose | \-\-dbg | \-\-debug ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR PATH
| \-\-field
.I PATH
| \-\-select
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-trace\-id
.IR HEX ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-V | \-\-version ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.\" =====================================================================
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pcloudc
is the command-line front end for the pCloud sync daemon: one binary to
sign in, manage sync folders and backups, mount your cloud drive, create
and share public links, drive the crypto folder, and inspect what the
daemon is doing. It is designed for day-to-day use from a terminal and
for scripting from shells and CI.
.PP
.B Status.
This client is the Rust rewrite of the legacy C
.BR pcloud-rs ,
currently in pre-alpha. Most control-plane surfaces are implemented,
but the mounted-drive / FUSE path is still under active development and
final parity has not been gated. Do not treat it as a drop-in
replacement for the C client in production.
.PP
.B Architecture (client/daemon split).
.B pcloudc
is a thin control client. A separate long-running daemon,
.BR pcloudd (1),
holds the session, performs HTTPS calls to the pCloud API, and drives
sync and mount workers.
.B pcloudc
reaches the daemon through a local IPC socket whose directory and file
modes are enforced owner-only (POSIX 0700 dir / 0600 socket; ACL-stubbed
on Windows). That means
.B pcloudc
commands never themselves talk to pcloud.com \(em if the daemon is not
running or unreachable you will get exit code 4
.RB ( Network )
or 6
.RB ( Unavailable ),
not a network error against the upstream service. Use
.B pcloudc start
to launch the daemon (or let your init system do it),
.B pcloudc status
to probe it, and
.B pcloudc doctor
to diagnose connectivity.
.PP
.B When to reach for pcloudc vs. other tools.
Use
.B pcloudc
for anything that changes daemon state: login, adding or removing sync
roots, creating public links, locking or unlocking crypto, mounting or
unmounting the cloud drive. Use ordinary shell tools
.RB ( ls (1),
.BR rsync (1),
editor of choice) against your already-synced local folder or a mounted
drive for day-to-day file work. Use
.BR jq (1)
on
.B pcloudc --json
output when you need to transform responses; the built-in
.B --field
selector (see
.BR "GLOBAL OPTIONS" )
covers the common case of pulling a single value out without a jq
dependency.
.PP
.B Your first minute.
.nf
    pcloudc start                           # launch the daemon
    pcloudc login \-\-user you@example.com     # sign in (password + TFA)
    pcloudc sync add ~/pCloud /             # mirror pCloud root locally
    pcloudc status                          # verify sync is healthy
    pcloudc \-\-json doctor                   # machine-readable self-check
.fi
.\" =====================================================================
.SH FIRST-RUN WALKTHROUGH
.IP 1. 4
Install
.B pcloud-rs
from your distribution or from the release tarball and make sure the
.B pcloudc
binary is on your
.IR PATH .
.IP 2. 4
Sign in:
.BR "pcloudc login \-\-user you@example.com"
(you will be prompted for your password and, if enabled, a two\-factor code).
.IP 3. 4
Add your first sync folder:
.BR "pcloudc sync add ~/pCloud /"
\- this mirrors your pCloud root into
.IR ~/pCloud .
.IP 4. 4
Check that the daemon is healthy and sync is running:
.BR "pcloudc status" .
.IP 5. 4
Optional: mount the cloud drive for on\-demand access with
.BR "pcloudc mount ~/pCloudDrive" .
.\" =====================================================================
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global flags are parsed before the subcommand and must appear
.B before
the subcommand name on the command line. They are extracted by
.I GlobalFlags::extract
.RI ( crates/pcloud-cli/src/globals.rs )
before the legacy positional parser runs, so anything placed after the
subcommand is treated as an argument to that subcommand, not as a global
flag. Unknown
.B \-\-foo
or
.B \-x
tokens are rejected with exit
.B 2
.RB ( Usage )
rather than silently dropped; a typo like
.B pcloudc \-\-qiet status
fails loudly instead of running
.B status
with defaults. When a rejected token has an attached value
.RB ( \-\-badtoken=sekret )
only the flag
.I name
is echoed back in the error, so accidentally-pasted secrets never leak
into logs.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the stable JSON envelope
.RB ( {"status":...,"message":...} )
on stdout instead of free-form human text.
.RS
.PP
.B Purpose.
Give scripts and CI a machine-parseable contract so they never have to
screen-scrape the human renderer, which is explicitly
.I not
parse-stable.
.PP
.B Why it exists.
The free-form text output is tuned for readability and changes between
releases. Parsing it with
.BR awk (1)
or
.BR sed (1)
is a time bomb; one wording change and your pipeline breaks.
.B \-\-json
locks in a documented schema you can pin on.
.PP
.B Beginner example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-json status" `.
.B Admin example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-json sync list | jq '.message.roots[] | select(.state!=\(dqok\(dq)'" `.
.PP
.B Interactions.
Equivalent to
.BR "\-\-output json" ;
the last of
.BR \-\-json / \-\-output
wins.
.B \-q/\-\-quiet
still suppresses the whole stdout stream (including the JSON envelope)
while preserving the exit code.
.PP
.B Common pitfall.
Putting
.B \-\-json
.I after
the subcommand
.RB ( "pcloudc status \-\-json" )
does not work: it will be parsed as a positional argument to
.B status
and the output stays text. Put global flags first.
.PP
.B Expert note.
In CI, prefer
.B \-\-json
piped to
.BR jq (1),
or
.B \-\-json --field <path>
for a single value, over parsing stderr. Stderr is reserved for
diagnostics (including the
.B [trace: ...]
line; see
.BR \-\-trace\-id )
and may carry warnings a script should not branch on.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-output " FORMAT"
Explicit output-format selector. Accepted values:
.BR text ", " human " (alias for " text "), " json .
.RS
.PP
.B Purpose.
Same effect as
.B \-\-json
but lets tooling that already has a
.B FORMAT
knob pass it through uniformly.
.PP
.B Why it exists.
The canonical form lives here; new formats (if ever added) would be a
breaking addition to
.I OutputFormat
and would require their own stability note. Keep scripts on
.B text
or
.B json
\(em nothing else will parse.
.PP
.B Beginner example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-output text status" `.
.B Admin example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-output json sync list" `.
.PP
.B Common pitfall.
.B \-\-output yaml
is rejected with
.BR "unknown value for --output" .
The renderer set is intentionally tiny.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the human-readable stdout stream.
.RS
.PP
.B Purpose.
Silence chatter in scripts and cron jobs while still letting the exit
code report success or failure.
.PP
.B Why it exists.
A script that wants only a yes/no outcome
.RB ( "if pcloudc resume; then ..." )
should not have to redirect stdout manually, and should not accidentally
hide a non-zero exit code.
.B \-\-quiet
mutes output but preserves exit semantics.
.PP
.B Beginner example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-q resume" `.
.B Admin example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-q \-\-config /etc/pcloud/work.conf sync pause || alert-script" `.
.PP
.B Interactions.
Does
.I not
affect stderr \(em error messages, the
.B [trace: ...]
correlation echo, and diagnostic logs still come through. Combine with
.B 2>/dev/null
only if you truly do not want any feedback on failure.
.PP
.B Common pitfall.
Combining
.B \-q
with
.B \-\-json
suppresses the JSON envelope too. If you want JSON for a script, omit
.BR \-q .
.RE
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-vv ", " \-vvv ", " \-\-verbose
Stackable verbosity. Maps to a tracing level hint: 0 (default)
.IR warn ,
1
.IR info ,
2
.IR debug ,
3+
.IR trace .
.B \-\-verbose
counts as one step and stacks with
.B \-v
occurrences; any stack longer than three saturates at trace.
.RS
.PP
.B Purpose.
Turn on increasing levels of CLI-side diagnostic output when something
is wrong.
.PP
.B Why it exists.
Noise at
.I info
or below is useless when you are debugging a transport-layer failure,
and a full
.I trace
stream is overwhelming when you just want to see what command was
dispatched. The stackable flag lets you pick an intensity per
invocation without editing config.
.PP
.B Beginner example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-v status" `
shows what the CLI is doing.
.B Admin example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-vvv sync add ~/Docs /Docs 2>sync-add.trace" `
captures a full trace for a support ticket.
.PP
.B Interactions.
Verbosity is client-side only; it does
.I not
change what the daemon logs. Use
.BR pcloudd (1)
log config for daemon-side verbosity.
.PP
.B Expert note.
In CI, prefer
.B \-v
over
.B \-vvv
to avoid blowing out log quotas. Reserve
.B \-vvv
for interactive reproduction of a bug.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-\-dbg ", " \-\-debug
Shortcut to maximum verbosity (equivalent to
.BR \-vvv ).
.RS
.PP
.B Purpose.
One-flag parity with the
.B \-\-debug
convention used by most dev tooling so muscle memory works.
.PP
.B Beginner example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-debug login" `.
.B Common pitfall.
.B \-\-debug
does not imply
.BR \-\-json ;
the stream is human-readable trace output. Combine explicitly if you
need machine-parseable diagnostics.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Point the CLI at an alternate
.BR pcloud.conf (5)
file.
.RS
.PP
.B Purpose.
Run multiple profiles side by side (personal vs. work, test vs.
production endpoint) without editing the default config.
.PP
.B Why it exists.
Config discovery order is: explicit
.BR \-\-config ,
then
.B $PCLOUD_CONFIG
(see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ),
then
.IR ~/.pcloud/config.toml .
Without a flag, scripts that want a non-default profile would have to
clobber the envvar for their shell.
.B \-\-config
is scoped to the single invocation.
.PP
.B Beginner example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-config ~/.pcloud/work.toml status" `.
.B Admin example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-config /etc/pcloud/prod.toml \-\-json sync list" `.
.PP
.B Common pitfall.
The daemon reads its own config at start time; changing
.B \-\-config
after the daemon is already running only affects
.B pcloudc
client behaviour (the daemon does not re-read on flag change). Restart
.B pcloudd
to pick up server-side config changes.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-f " " \fIPATH\fR ", " \-\-field " " \fIPATH\fR ", " \-\-select " " \fIPATH\fR
Project a single dotted-path field out of the response
.BR message .
Repeatable; each occurrence adds another path, printed in order.
.RS
.PP
.B Purpose.
Pull one or a handful of values out of a response without installing or
piping to
.BR jq (1).
Cuts a
.RB ` "pcloudc ... | jq \-r .foo" `
pipeline down to a single call.
.PP
.B Why it exists.
The daemon response envelope is stable
.RB ( status " + " message ),
and many callers just want a single field
.RB ( quota ,
.BR email ,
.BR count ).
Shelling out to
.BR jq (1)
is surprisingly heavy in container images, and the selector is enforced
at render time inside the CLI \(em which guarantees it can never project
a secret-bearing field, because the selector only walks values already
sanitised by the daemon.
.PP
.B Syntax
(a tiny jq subset):
.IP \(bu 2
.B key
\(em object field lookup.
.IP \(bu 2
.B key.sub.leaf
\(em dotted nested keys.
.IP \(bu 2
.B list.0.id
\(em numeric segments are array indices.
.IP \(bu 2
.B .
or the empty string \(em selects the whole value (useful to re-render a
non-JSON message as parsed JSON).
.IP \(bu 2
Leading dot is tolerated
.RB ( .quota " \(eq " quota ).
.PP
Filters, pipes, slices, comma splits,
.BR @csv ,
assignment \(em anything jq does beyond plain path selection \(em are
not supported. If you need those, pipe
.B \-\-json
output to
.BR jq (1).
.PP
.B Accepted message shapes.
The selector accepts any of three response-message shapes:
.IP \(bu 2
Real JSON
.RB ( {\(dqquota\(dq:\ 10737418240} ).
.IP \(bu 2
Legacy flat
.B key=value
lists (with optional
.BR command: " prefix),"
e.g.
.BR "userinfo: quota=10737418240, premium=false, email=\(dqa@b\(dq" .
.IP \(bu 2
Plain text \(em only the empty selector
.RB ( . )
matches.
.PP
.B Beginner examples:
.nf
    pcloudc userinfo \-\-field quota
    pcloudc userinfo \-f email
    pcloudc list-links \-\-field links.0.id \-\-field count
.fi
.B Admin examples:
.nf
    # Alert when free quota drops below 10 GiB.
    used=$(pcloudc userinfo \-\-field usedquota)
    total=$(pcloudc userinfo \-\-field quota)
    (( total - used < 10*1024**3 )) && alert quota-low

    # Pull a structured sub-object back out as JSON.
    pcloudc \-\-json status \-\-field message.sync

    # Build a CSV-ish line from multiple fields.
    printf '%s,%s\\n' \\
        "$(pcloudc userinfo \-\-field email)" \\
        "$(pcloudc userinfo \-\-field quota)"
.fi
.PP
.B Output form.
With a single
.B \-\-field
the value is rendered unquoted (strings verbatim, numbers and booleans
as themselves, nulls as the empty string, arrays/objects fall back to
compact JSON). With multiple
.B \-\-field
paths, each value is printed on its own line in the order given.
.PP
.B Interactions.
Combining
.B \-\-field
with
.B \-\-json
is supported and renders a JSON object keyed by the requested paths.
If any path is missing or hits a type mismatch the command exits
.B 2
.RB ( Usage )
with an error listing the available sibling keys at the failing step.
.PP
.B Common pitfalls.
Object keys that happen to be numeric
.RB ( \(dq0\(dq ", " \(dq1\(dq )
cannot be disambiguated from array indices by the parser; a segment
like
.B 0
is always parsed as
.BR Index(0) .
If you hit this limitation, use
.B \-\-json
and pipe to
.BR jq (1).
The selector is
.I case-sensitive
and matches the daemon's field naming exactly \(em
.B quota
succeeds where
.B Quota
does not.
.PP
.B Expert note.
In CI pipelines, prefer
.B "\-\-json \-\-field <path>"
over
.B "\-\-json | jq"
when you only need one value: you avoid the
.BR jq (1)
container-image footprint, the selector cannot accidentally project a
secret, and the CLI returns a clean exit
.B 2
if the path does not match (vs.
.BR jq 's
.B null
default, which silently produces the wrong data).
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-trace\-id " HEX"
Correlate this invocation with a W3C Trace Context
.IR trace-id .
The value must be a 32-character lowercase hexadecimal string.
.RS
.PP
.B Purpose.
Thread a single distributed-trace identity through the CLI, the daemon
dispatch, the per-backend handler, and the upstream pCloud API call so
one click in your tracing backend reconstructs the full path.
.PP
.B Why it exists.
Without a pinned trace id, the daemon decides whether to start a fresh
root span based on its head
.BR sample_rate .
That is fine for ambient traffic but useless when a user reports a bug:
you have no way to find
.I their
request in the trace store. An explicit
.B \-\-trace\-id
force-samples the invocation
.RB ( flags\ =\ 01 )
so the backend always stores it.
.PP
.B Behaviour.
When
.B \-\-trace\-id
is given, the CLI synthesises a fresh non-zero 16-byte span id and
prints the canonical traceparent line once to
.B stderr
before the command result:
.PP
.nf
    [trace: 00\-<32 hex trace id>\-<16 hex span id>\-01]
.fi
.PP
When
.B \-\-trace\-id
is absent, the CLI falls back to the
.B TRACEPARENT
environment variable
.RB ( "00-<trace:32hex>-<span:16hex>-<flags:2hex>" );
malformed values are dropped silently. When neither is set the request
goes out without a traceparent and the daemon applies its configured
sampling policy.
.PP
.B Beginner example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-trace\-id 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736 status" `.
.B Admin example:
.nf
    TRACEPARENT=00\-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\-00f067aa0ba902b7\-01 \\
        pcloudc sync add ~/Docs /Docs
.fi
.PP
.B Interactions.
.B \-\-trace\-id
takes precedence over
.B TRACEPARENT
when both are present.
.B \-q/\-\-quiet
suppresses the
.B [trace: ...]
stderr echo but does not change what is sent on the wire.
.PP
.B Common pitfalls.
Only lowercase hex is accepted; uppercase
.RB ( 4BF92F... )
and non-hex input are rejected with exit
.BR 2 .
The flag value never appears in error output if parsing fails \(em
malformed trace ids are reported as
.B "invalid --trace-id value"
without echoing the offending string.
.PP
.B Expert note.
Wire this up to your support-ticket template: have users paste the
.B [trace: ...]
line from stderr into the bug report and the on-call engineer can jump
straight from tracker to trace store. See
.B OBSERVABILITY / DISTRIBUTED TRACING
below for the full cross-process span layout.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage, the global-flag list, the subcommand list, and the
documented exit-code table.
.RS
.PP
.B Purpose.
Self-contained reference so users never have to cross-check source or
this manpage for the basics.
.PP
.B Beginner example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-help" `.
.B Admin example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-help | grep \-A1 '^EXIT CODES'" `
to pull the exit-code legend into a runbook.
.PP
.B Common pitfall.
Per-subcommand help lives behind
.BR ` "pcloudc <command> \-\-help" `,
not the global
.BR \-\-help .
The global form is intentionally short.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and build metadata and exit
.BR 0 .
.RS
.PP
.B Purpose.
Let scripts, telemetry, and support staff identify which build is
actually installed \(em critical when multiple packages are in play.
.PP
.B Beginner example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-version" `.
.B Admin example:
.RB ` "pcloudc \-\-version | awk '{print $2}'" `
to capture the semver into a deployment log.
.PP
.B Common pitfall.
The client version and the running-daemon version can differ after an
upgrade.
.B pcloudc status
reports the daemon version; cross-check both when filing a bug.
.RE
.PP
.B Flag-parser safety notes.
The parser treats
.B \-
(a bare dash) as a positional argument, not an unknown flag \(em
this matches the stdin convention used by many tools. Tokens attached
with
.B =
.RB ( \-\-config=/path )
are supported alongside the space-separated form
.RB ( "\-\-config /path" ).
Secret-bearing values on the command line are not a supported input
channel; use
.BR \-\-password\-stdin ,
.BR \-\-password\-env ,
or the interactive prompt instead (see
.B login
under
.BR "Signing in" ).
.\" =====================================================================
.SH COMMANDS
Commands are grouped by what you are trying to do. Run
.B pcloudc <command> \-\-help
for the latest option list on any command.
.SS Daemon lifecycle
.TP
.B start
.B When to use this:
to launch the background daemon yourself (usually your init system does this).
.RS
.nf
pcloudc start
pcloudc \-\-debug start
.fi
You get back: a running daemon listening on the local IPC socket.
.RE
.TP
.BR stop ", " finalize ", " shutdown
.B When to use this:
to ask the daemon to exit cleanly.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc stop
pcloudc shutdown
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BR reload ", " daemon-reload
.B Purpose:
send SIGHUP to the running daemon to trigger a config hot-reload.
.B When to use this:
after editing the on-disk config file to change log level, rate-limit
budgets, integrity-sweeper schedule, sync poll interval, or
data-residency allow-list without restarting the daemon. Auth vault
path, IPC socket path, and crypto master key changes still require a
full restart.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc reload
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B status
.B Purpose:
one\-shot liveness + session summary for the running daemon.
.B Why it exists:
scripts and humans both need a fast "is everything OK" answer that covers
transport, session, and sync engine at once; this is the command most CI jobs
cron against.
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc [\-\-json] status" .
No positional arguments, no flags of its own. Works pre\-auth (you still see
the daemon state and transport reachability; the
.B session
block will report
.I unauthenticated
until you log in).
.br
.B Output shape:
plaintext
.I "status: ..."
one\-liners by default; with
.B \-\-json
a JSON object with top\-level keys:
.IR daemon ", "
.IR transport ", "
.IR session ", "
.IR syncs ", "
.IR transfers .
.RS
.nf
pcloudc status
pcloudc \-\-json status
pcloudc \-\-json status session.authenticated  # field selector
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "daemon unreachable"
(start the daemon with
.B pcloudc start
or via systemd),
.IR "transport down"
(check network / see
.BR health ),
.IR "unauthenticated"
(run
.BR login ).
.B Related:
.BR health ", "
.BR session-status ", "
.BR doctor .
.TP
.B health
.B Purpose:
detailed transport + worker probe. Complements
.B status
with per\-subsystem numbers (transport RTT, queue depths, worker counts,
vault reachability).
.B Why it exists:
when
.B status
shows something wrong,
.B health
tells you which subsystem owns the failure. Also the right command for
Prometheus/heartbeat scrapers that need structured fields.
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc [\-\-json] health" .
No positional arguments. Works pre\-auth.
.B Output shape:
plaintext status lines, or JSON object with keys such as
.IR transport ", "
.IR queues ", "
.IR workers ", "
.IR vault ", "
.IR uptime_seconds .
.RS
.nf
pcloudc health
pcloudc \-\-json health
pcloudc \-\-json health transport.reachable uptime_seconds
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "transport unreachable"
(TLS/DNS/firewall; check
.BR PCLOUD_API_HOST ),
.IR "queue backlog"
(transient; pause/resume or wait),
.IR "vault unreadable"
(run
.BR doctor ).
.B Related:
.BR status ", "
.BR doctor ", "
.BR session-status .
.TP
.B doctor
.B When to use this:
automated self\-diagnosis when something is wrong. Always try this before
filing a bug. Cross\-platform; probes adapt to the host OS.
Options:
.BR "\-\-strict "
(treat any non\-fatal warning as failure and exit
.BR 6 ;
intended for CI and wave gates).
.PP
.B Probe matrix:
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
Probe@Linux/macOS/BSD@Windows
vault\-perms@POSIX 0600 file / 0700 dir@NTFS ACL stub (owner\-only)
disk\-free@\fBstatvfs\fR(3) on cache_dir@\fBGetDiskFreeSpaceExW\fR
clock\-drift@NTP/chrony offset, 30s threshold@W32Time offset, 30s threshold
config/runtime dir mode@POSIX mode bits@ACL stub
IPC endpoint@unix socket reachable@named pipe reachable
daemon binary@\fBPATH\fR lookup@\fB%PATH%\fR lookup
.TE
.RS
.nf
pcloudc doctor
pcloudc \-\-json doctor
pcloudc doctor \-\-strict
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B session-status
.B Purpose:
show just the authentication + crypto portion of daemon state:
whether the session is authenticated, which user is attached, whether
the crypto folder is locked, whether the auth token is persisted.
.B Why it exists:
.B status
bundles transport and sync alongside auth;
.B session-status
lets scripts and the
.B login
state machine probe only the auth half without parsing the whole status
tree. It is the canonical "am I logged in?" oracle.
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc [\-\-json] session-status"
(alias:
.BR "pcloudc session status" ).
No positional arguments. Works pre\-auth; reports
.I unauthenticated
then.
.B Output shape:
JSON with at minimum
.IR authenticated ", "
.IR username ", "
.IR tfa_pending ", "
.IR crypto_locked ", "
.IR token_persisted .
.RS
.nf
pcloudc session-status
pcloudc \-\-json session-status
pcloudc \-\-json session-status authenticated tfa_pending
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "tfa_pending=true"
(complete 2FA via
.B submit-tfa
or
.BR submit-recovery ),
.IR "authenticated=false"
(run
.BR login ),
.IR "crypto_locked=true"
(run
.B unlock-crypto
for encrypted folders).
.B Related:
.BR login ", "
.BR logout ", "
.BR submit-tfa ", "
.BR status ", "
.BR authsave .
.SS Signing in
.PP
Authentication on the daemon is a state machine, not a single call. A fresh
daemon starts in
.I unauthenticated
state. The high\-level
.B login
command drives the whole flow (password, 2FA if required, optional token
persistence); the
.B submit-*
and
.B send-tfa-*
commands are the individual steps, exposed so scripts, CI jobs, and
headless hosts can advance the state machine without interactive prompts.
.PP
.B Flow diagram (typical 2FA account):
.nf
    login \-\-user EMAIL
        \\_ submit-password       (username + password \(-> API)
            \\_ if TFA required:
                \\_ send-tfa-sms          (push OTP to phone)    OR
                \\_ send-tfa-notification (push to pCloud app)   OR
                \\_ (nothing; user already has a TOTP code)
                \\_ submit-tfa CODE       (6\-digit OTP)          OR
                \\_ submit-recovery CODE  (one\-time backup code)
            \\_ session = authenticated
        \\_ optional: authsave           (persist token to vault)
.fi
.PP
.B When to use which 2FA channel:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B sms
\(em account has a phone number on file and the user prefers SMS. Resend
with
.BR send-tfa-sms
if the message does not arrive within ~30 s.
.IP \(bu 2
.B notification
\(em user has the pCloud mobile app; a push appears they must approve.
Resend with
.BR send-tfa-notification .
.IP \(bu 2
.B code
\(em user already has a TOTP generator (authenticator app) and types the
6\-digit code. No send\-side command is needed; go straight to
.BR submit-tfa .
.IP \(bu 2
.B recovery
\(em user lost their 2FA device and uses one of the printed backup codes.
Submitted via
.BR submit-recovery .
Single\-use per code.
.RE
.TP
.B login
.B Purpose:
end\-to\-end authentication driver. Prompts for whatever is missing
(username, password, TFA code) and leaves the daemon in the
.I authenticated
state.
.B Why it exists:
for humans and for scripts that want one command to cover the whole flow.
Internally it invokes
.BR submit-password ,
optionally
.BR send-tfa-sms / send-tfa-notification ,
then
.BR submit-tfa / submit-recovery ,
and (if
.B \-\-save-password
is set) finally
.BR authsave .
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc login [\-\-user EMAIL] [\-\-password-stdin | \-\-password-env VAR] [\-T CHANNEL] [\-r] [\-s]" .
.B Flags:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "\-\-user, \-u " \fIEMAIL\fR " / " "\-\-username " \fIEMAIL\fR
\(em account email. Optional; prompts if omitted. Validated as a non\-empty
string; the API rejects malformed addresses.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-password-stdin
\(em read password from stdin. RECOMMENDED for scripts. Mutually exclusive
with
.BR \-\-password-env .
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "\-\-password-env " \fIVAR\fR
\(em read password from the named environment variable. Mutually exclusive
with
.BR \-\-password-stdin .
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "\-\-tfa-channel, \-T " \fIsms|notification|code|recovery\fR
\(em tell
.B login
which 2FA path to drive. Optional; if omitted and the account requires
2FA,
.B login
prompts interactively.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "\-\-trusted-device, \-r "
\(em mark this device as trusted so future logins skip 2FA. Optional
boolean.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "\-\-save-password, \-s "
\(em after success, persist the returned auth token via
.B authsave
so the next run skips the whole flow.
.RE
.B Authentication requirements:
pre\-auth (this is how you authenticate).
.B Output shape:
plaintext progress lines on the default transport; with
.B \-\-json
a JSON object describing the final session (see
.BR session-status ).
.RS
.nf
# 1) Interactive, easiest:
pcloudc login \-\-user alice@example.com

# 2) Scripted, password from stdin, SMS 2FA, trust device:
printf '%s' "$PASS" | pcloudc login \\
    \-\-user alice@example.com \-\-password-stdin \-T sms \-r

# 3) Troubleshooting a stuck 2FA prompt:
pcloudc \-\-debug login \-\-user alice@example.com
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "invalid credentials"
(re\-check password; use the web dashboard's
.I lost_password
flow if needed),
.IR "TFA required"
(re\-run with
.BR "\-T sms" ,
.BR "\-T notification" ,
or provide the code interactively),
.IR "rate-limited"
(API back\-off; wait and retry).
.B Related:
.BR logout ", "
.BR submit-password ", "
.BR submit-tfa ", "
.BR authsave ", "
.BR session-status .
.TP
.B logout
.B Purpose:
drop the in\-memory session and optionally invalidate the server\-side
token.
.B Why it exists:
to end the session without killing the daemon. Complements
.B authsave off
(which only stops persistence across restarts);
.B logout
also clears the current process's session.
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc logout" .
No positional arguments, no flags.
.B Authentication requirements:
requires an active session (no\-op otherwise).
.B Output shape:
single plaintext line; with
.B \-\-json
a JSON object with
.IR logged_out: true .
.RS
.nf
pcloudc logout
pcloudc \-\-json logout
pcloudc logout && pcloudc session-status        # verify
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "not authenticated"
(already logged out; harmless),
.IR "daemon unreachable"
(start the daemon first).
.B Related:
.BR login ", "
.BR authsave ", "
.BR session-status .
.TP
.B submit-password
.B Purpose:
single\-step password submission. Advances the login state machine from
.I awaiting-password
to either
.I authenticated
or
.IR tfa-required .
.B Why it exists:
.B login
handles the whole flow interactively; scripts and headless jobs often need
to feed the password separately from the username (for example, username
from a provisioning system, password from a secrets manager). Also the
building block the legacy
.B auth <password>
alias maps to.
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc submit-password [USERNAME] [\-\-password-stdin | \-\-password-env VAR]" .
Positional
.I USERNAME
is optional; prompts if omitted. Argv passwords are accepted for backward
compatibility but emit a stderr warning because they are visible to other
processes via
.BR /proc/<pid>/cmdline ;
use
.BR \-\-password-stdin
or
.BR \-\-password-env
instead.
.B Authentication requirements:
pre\-auth.
.B Output shape:
JSON object with
.IR authenticated ", "
.IR tfa_required ", "
.IR tfa_type
(when applicable).
.RS
.nf
# Interactive:
pcloudc submit-password alice@example.com

# Scripted:
printf '%s' "$PASS" | pcloudc submit-password alice@example.com \-\-password-stdin

# From a secret manager via env:
PCLOUD_PW="$(vault kv get \-field=pw pcloud)" \\
    pcloudc submit-password alice@example.com \-\-password-env PCLOUD_PW
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "bad password"
(retry),
.IR "tfa_required"
(proceed to
.BR send-tfa-sms / send-tfa-notification " then " submit-tfa ),
.IR "argv password warning"
(switch to
.BR \-\-password-stdin ).
.B Related:
.BR login ", "
.BR submit-auth ", "
.BR submit-tfa ", "
.BR session-status .
.TP
.B submit-auth
.B Purpose:
submit a pre\-existing API auth token instead of a password. Shortcuts the
password step entirely.
.B Why it exists:
service accounts, CI runners, and restored vault handoffs already hold a
valid token; making them replay the password dance would add attack surface
and friction. This command lets them authenticate directly.
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc submit-auth [TOKEN]" .
Prompts (no echo) when
.I TOKEN
is omitted. Because the token is as sensitive as the password, avoid
placing it on argv; prompt or read from a protected source.
.B Authentication requirements:
pre\-auth.
.B Output shape:
JSON object with
.IR authenticated ", "
.IR username .
.RS
.nf
pcloudc submit-auth                             # interactive
pcloudc submit-auth "$PCLOUD_TOKEN"             # CI runner
pcloudc submit-auth && pcloudc authsave         # persist for next boot
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "invalid token"
(token expired or revoked; re\-run
.BR login ),
.IR "tfa_required"
(tokens typically bypass 2FA, but if forwarded, follow the TFA path).
.B Related:
.BR login ", "
.BR submit-password ", "
.BR authsave .
.TP
.B submit-tfa
.B Purpose:
submit a 6\-digit two\-factor code (TOTP/SMS/push) to complete a login that
is in
.I tfa-required
state.
.B Why it exists:
separating password submission from 2FA submission lets scripts and the
interactive prompt share the same backend contract, and lets the daemon
preserve the TFA\-pending state across client reconnects.
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc submit-tfa [CODE] [\-\-trust-device]"
(legacy alias:
.BR "pcloudc tfa CODE" ).
Prompts (no echo) when
.I CODE
is omitted.
.B \-\-trust-device
marks this machine as trusted so subsequent logins skip 2FA.
.B Authentication requirements:
requires that a prior
.B submit-password
(or
.BR login )
left the daemon in
.I tfa-required
state.
.B Output shape:
JSON object with
.IR authenticated ", "
.IR trusted_device .
.RS
.nf
pcloudc submit-tfa 123456
pcloudc submit-tfa 123456 \-\-trust-device
pcloudc tfa 123456                              # legacy alias
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "wrong or expired code"
(codes are typically valid for ~30 s; resend with
.BR send-tfa-sms ),
.IR "no pending TFA"
(run
.B submit-password
first),
.IR "rate-limited"
(wait, then retry).
.B Related:
.BR submit-recovery ", "
.BR send-tfa-sms ", "
.BR send-tfa-notification ", "
.BR login .
.TP
.B submit-recovery
.B Purpose:
submit a one\-time recovery (backup) code in place of the normal 2FA code.
Used when the 2FA device is lost or unavailable.
.B Why it exists:
recovery codes are a separate credential class on the server side (they are
single\-use and bypass the 2FA device) and therefore need their own submit
path. Exists as a distinct command so auditing and rate\-limits can treat
recovery submissions differently from normal TFA.
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc submit-recovery [CODE] [\-\-trust-device]" .
Prompts (no echo) when
.I CODE
is omitted.
.B Authentication requirements:
requires a pending
.I tfa-required
state (same as
.BR submit-tfa ).
.B Output shape:
JSON object with
.IR authenticated ", "
.IR recovery_codes_remaining
(when the server returns it).
.RS
.nf
pcloudc submit-recovery ABCD-EFGH-1234
pcloudc submit-recovery                                      # interactive
pcloudc \-\-json submit-recovery ABCD-EFGH-1234 recovery_codes_remaining
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "code already used"
(each recovery code is single\-use; try the next one),
.IR "invalid code"
(check for typos; recovery codes are case\-sensitive),
.IR "no pending TFA"
(run
.B submit-password
first).
.B Related:
.BR submit-tfa ", "
.BR send-tfa-sms ", "
.BR login .
.TP
.B send-tfa-sms
.B Purpose:
ask the pCloud API to (re)send the SMS 2FA OTP to the phone number on file.
.B Why it exists:
SMS delivery is unreliable; a dedicated resend command is the only correct
way to request another OTP without abandoning the pending login state and
starting over (which would reset server\-side rate counters against you).
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc send-tfa-sms" .
No positional arguments, no flags.
.B Authentication requirements:
requires a pending
.I tfa-required
state (a prior
.B submit-password
that returned
.IR "tfa_type = sms" ).
.B Output shape:
JSON object with
.IR sent: true ", "
.IR tfa_type: "\"sms\""
on success.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc send-tfa-sms
pcloudc send-tfa-sms && pcloudc submit-tfa 654321
pcloudc \-\-json send-tfa-sms sent tfa_type
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "no pending TFA"
(start with
.BR submit-password ),
.IR "rate-limited"
(SMS resends are rate\-limited server\-side; wait),
.IR "wrong tfa_type"
(account is configured for push, not SMS; use
.BR send-tfa-notification
instead).
.B Related:
.BR send-tfa-notification ", "
.BR submit-tfa ", "
.BR login .
.TP
.B send-tfa-notification
.B Purpose:
ask the API to (re)push the 2FA approval notification to the pCloud mobile
app.
.B Why it exists:
push notifications can be missed or dismissed; a dedicated resend path
keeps the pending\-login state intact while re\-triggering the push.
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc send-tfa-notification" .
No positional arguments, no flags.
.B Authentication requirements:
requires a pending
.I tfa-required
state with
.IR "tfa_type = notification" .
.B Output shape:
JSON object with
.IR sent: true ", "
.IR tfa_type: "\"notification\"" .
.RS
.nf
pcloudc send-tfa-notification
pcloudc send-tfa-notification && pcloudc submit-tfa 112233
pcloudc \-\-json send-tfa-notification
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "mobile app not installed"
(the notification channel requires the app; fall back to SMS or recovery),
.IR "rate-limited"
(wait),
.IR "no pending TFA"
(start with
.BR submit-password ).
.B Related:
.BR send-tfa-sms ", "
.BR submit-tfa ", "
.BR login .
.TP
.B authsave \fR[\fIoff\fR]
.B Purpose:
persist the current session's auth token to an owner\-only on\-disk vault so
future runs (including reboots) skip the whole login flow.
.B Why it exists:
re\-entering a password on every daemon restart is a poor experience, but
persisting a password in clear is insecure. The compromise is to persist
the API token (never the password) in an
.I opt-in ,
owner\-only file with strict permissions. Bare
.B authsave
enables it;
.BI authsave " off"
disables it and wipes the token.
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc authsave " [\fIoff\fR|\fIfalse\fR|\fI0\fR|\fIno\fR|\fIdisable\fR]
.br
No argument \(-> enable. Any of the disable aliases \(-> disable and purge.
.B Authentication requirements:
requires an active session for
.I enable
(there is nothing to save otherwise);
.I disable
works even without a session.
.B Security tradeoff:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The vault file is created
.B 0600
(owner read/write only); its parent directory is
.BR 0700 .
Any deviation is treated as tamper and the daemon refuses to load it.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the API
.I token
is persisted, never the password.
.IP \(bu 2
The vault lives under
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/pcloudc/
(typically
.IR ~/.local/state/pcloudc/ );
see
.BR pcloud.conf (5)
and the
.B FILES
section below for the exact path on your platform.
.IP \(bu 2
Anyone with root, or the same UID, on the host effectively has your
session. Treat the vault as sensitive as a password.
.RE
.B Output shape:
JSON object with
.IR persisted: true|false ", "
.IR path
(when persisted).
.RS
.nf
pcloudc authsave                                # enable
pcloudc authsave off                            # disable and wipe
pcloudc login \-s \-\-user alice@example.com      # login + authsave in one
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "not authenticated"
(run
.B login
first),
.IR "permission refused on vault dir"
(run
.BR doctor " \(em it checks 0600/0700 "
and will report the bad mode),
.IR "I/O error"
(disk full or read\-only; check
.BR df ).
.B Related:
.BR login ", "
.BR logout ", "
.BR session-status ", "
.BR doctor ", "
.BR pcloud.conf (5).
.PP
.B Account\-lifecycle operations not exposed as subcommands:
the server\-side operations
.IR verify_email ", "
.IR verify_email_restricted ", "
.IR lost_password ", "
.IR change_password ", "
.IR get_promo ", "
.IR get_api_servers ", "
.IR set_language ", and "
.IR set_api_server
are available through the pCloud SDK and the daemon runtime, but they are
.B not
surfaced as dedicated
.B pcloudc
subcommands in this release. Use the pCloud web dashboard for
.IR verify_email / lost_password / change_password ,
and the pCloud client SDK for the rest. If the daemon is invoked for one
of these on a build where the handler is not wired, it returns
.BR Unavailable
(see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ).
There is no
.B whoami
subcommand; use
.B userinfo
or
.BR session-status .
.SS Sync folders
.PP
.B Concept primer \- sync vs mount.
A
.I sync root
is a bidirectional pairing between a local directory and a remote pCloud
folder: the daemon keeps both sides in step by
.B copying
bytes in each direction on its own schedule (and on demand via
.BR "sync localscan" ).
Files on the local side are real files on your disk; they work offline and
another process reading them pays no network cost.
.PP
A
.I mount
(next subsection) is different: it projects the remote drive into a FUSE
mount point so that reading a file fetches it on demand with no durable
local copy. Use
.B sync
when you want offline availability and predictable local copies; use
.B mount
when you want remote files to appear everywhere without consuming local
disk. The two surfaces are independent \- a local path can sit under a sync
root or under a mountpoint, but never under both simultaneously; the daemon
rejects nested or duplicate sync roots during
.BR "sync add" .
.PP
All
.B sync
subcommands require a running daemon. Unless noted otherwise they also
require an authenticated session
.RB ( "pcloudc login" );
if the daemon socket is unreachable the CLI exits with
.BR Unavailable .
.SS "Sync flavors (directions)"
Every sync root has a direction flavor. The CLI accepts nine
case-insensitive aliases grouped into three families (legacy C
.BR psync_synctype_t ):
.PP
.RS
.B bilateral \fR|\fR full \fR|\fR both
.RS 4
Two-way reconciliation. Local deletions propagate to the remote, remote
deletions propagate to the local. This is the default for
.B sync add
when
.B \-\-type
is not supplied, mirroring C
.B PSYNC_FULL .
Use for interactive workstation workflows.
.RE
.B mirror \fR|\fR download-only \fR|\fR down \fR|\fR remote-to-local
.RS 4
One-way remote \(-> local. Local edits are never uploaded; a remote
deletion removes the local copy. Mirrors C
.B PSYNC_DOWNLOAD_ONLY .
Use to keep a read-only replica of a shared folder.
.RE
.B upload-only \fR|\fR up \fR|\fR local-to-remote
.RS 4
One-way local \(-> remote. Remote edits are never downloaded. Mirrors C
.B PSYNC_UPLOAD_ONLY .
A local deletion
.B DOES
propagate to the remote (destructive mirror). Use this flavor only when
you explicitly want the legacy behaviour; most users who say "backup"
want the deletion-safe
.B backup
flavor below instead.
.RE
.B backup \fR|\fR backup-archive \fR|\fR archive \fR|\fR keep-remote
.RS 4
Deletion-safe archival. Uploads new and changed local files like
.BR upload-only ,
but a local deletion
.B does NOT
delete the remote copy \- the archived remote copy survives. Remote
changes are still not downloaded. This is a Rust-only flavor with no
legacy C
.B psync_synctype_t
counterpart (persisted as numeric code 4; tracked under
.BR "bd-1du.5 Deletion-safe backup sync flavor" ).
If you need a one-shot point-in-time archive (zstd\-compressed,
SHA3\-256\-stamped tarball) instead of a continuously-replicated tree,
use
.B "snapshot create"
\- see the
.B SNAPSHOTS
section.
.RE
.RE
.PP
The direction of an existing root can be changed without re-adding it;
see
.BR "sync change-type" .
Direction changes evict any already-queued upload/download work
because the plan may no longer be valid; the next scan cycle rebuilds
the queue.
.TP
.B sync list
.RB "(aliases: " "sync ls" ", " "s ls" ", " sync-list )
.B Purpose:
enumerate every configured sync root the daemon knows about.
.br
.B Why it exists:
.B sync add
does not print its persistent id on success; any script that later needs to
pause, remove, or inspect a root recovers the numeric
.I sync_id
and the daemon's canonicalised local/remote path through
.BR "sync list" .
It is also how you confirm that a previous
.B sync add
was persisted.
.br
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc sync list"
\- no positionals, no subcommand flags.
.br
.B Preconditions:
daemon reachable. Works on the local sync graph and does not strictly
require an authenticated session; on a fresh install the list is empty.
.br
.B Output shape:
one row per sync root with (at minimum)
.BR sync_id ", " local_path ", " remote_folder_id ", " remote_path ", " paused .
Under
.B \-\-json
the array lives at
.IR result.sync_roots[] ;
project fields with
.B \-f
(e.g.
.BR "\-f sync_roots.0.sync_id" ).
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync list                                         # beginner
pcloudc \-\-json sync list | jq '.result.sync_roots[].sync_id'    # scripted
pcloudc \-\-json \-f sync_roots sync list                 # troubleshooting
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR Unavailable " - daemon socket missing;"
.BR Unauthorized " - auth vault unreadable."
.br
.B Related:
.BR "sync add" ", " "sync remove" ", " pending .
.TP
.B sync add \fILOCAL\fR \fIREMOTE\fR \fR[\fB\-\-type\fR \fIFLAVOR\fR]
.RB "(alias: " sync-add )
.B Purpose:
register a new sync pairing and start reconciling the two sides. The
default direction is bilateral; pass
.B \-\-type
to create a mirror-only or upload-only root. See the
.B Sync flavors
subsection above for the full alias table and the pre-alpha honesty
caveat about the
.B backup
alias.
.br
.B Why it exists:
adding a sync root is more than a config edit. The daemon performs the full
lifecycle on your behalf: (1) canonicalise
.I LOCAL
to an absolute path following symlinks, (2) reject the request if
.I LOCAL
is already a sync root, is nested inside one, or contains one, (3)
validate that
.I REMOTE
exists on the account via backend folder listing, (4) persist the pairing
in the local SQLite store, (5) enqueue the initial reconcile work on the
scheduler. This pipeline is what differentiates
.B sync add
from
.B mount
\- the latter never copies bytes to disk.
.br
.B Syntax + positionals:
.BR "pcloudc sync add " \fILOCAL\fR " " \fIREMOTE\fR " [\fB\-\-type\fR " \fIFLAVOR\fR ].
.RS
.TP 4
.I LOCAL
absolute or
.B ~
-expanded local directory that already exists. Type: filesystem path.
Validation: must resolve, must be a directory, must not overlap another
sync root. The daemon does not create the directory for you.
.TP 4
.I REMOTE
absolute pCloud-drive path (for example
.BR /Documents ).
Type: string. Validation: must exist on the account; relative paths are
rejected.
.TP 4
\fB\-\-type\fR \fIFLAVOR\fR
optional direction flavor. Accepted aliases are listed in the
.B Sync flavors
subsection above. Default when omitted:
.B bilateral
(C
.BR PSYNC_FULL ).
Unknown aliases are rejected with
.BR InvalidRequest ;
the error message enumerates the full 9-alias list. Note that
.B backup
is currently an alias for
.B upload-only
and does propagate local deletions \- see the caveat above.
.RE
If either positional is omitted the CLI falls back to an interactive
prompt.
.I --type
is only consulted when supplied; there is no interactive prompt for it
today.
.br
.B Preconditions:
authenticated session;
.I LOCAL
owned by the invoking user; the remote path resolvable by the backend
.BR getfolderbypath .
.br
.B Output shape:
the daemon returns a structured JSON payload in the response
.I message
(ADR-0017):
.BR sync_id ", " local_path ", " remote_path ", " remote_folder_id ", " sync_type .
The
.B sync_type
field is the canonical variant name
.RB ( Full ", " UploadOnly ", or " DownloadOnly "),"
not an alias. Under
.BR \-\-json ,
.BR result.sync_id " / " result.sync_type " / " result.local_path
are the useful selectors; with
.B \-\-field
you can project any of them without a JSON parser, e.g.
.BR "\-f sync_id \-f sync_type" .
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync add ~/Documents /Documents                   # beginner, bilateral
pcloudc sync add ~/Photos /Photos --type mirror           # remote -> local only
pcloudc sync add ~/archive /Archive --type backup         # local -> remote only
                                                          # (see caveat above)
pcloudc \-\-json sync add ~/Photos /Backups/Photos \\
    --type download-only | jq '{id:.result.sync_id, kind:.result.sync_type}'
pcloudc \-f sync_id \-f sync_type sync add ~/a /a         # field selector, no jq
pcloudc \-\-json sync add ~/Photos /Backups/Photos 2>&1 \\
    | tee /tmp/sync-add.log                                # troubleshooting
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR Conflict " - duplicate or nested sync root;"
.BR NotFound " - remote path does not exist (create it with " "folder create" ");"
.BR InvalidRequest " - local path missing, not a directory, relative, or unknown \-\-type FLAVOR;"
.BR Unauthorized " - not logged in."
.br
.B Related:
.BR "folder create" ", " "folder id" ", " "sync list" ", " "sync remove" ", " "sync change-type" ", " "snapshot create" .
.TP
.B sync remove \fISYNC-ID\fR
.RB "(aliases: " "sync rm" ", " "s rm" ", " sync-remove )
.B Purpose:
unregister a sync root. Local files are
.B not
deleted; the daemon stops reconciling them and evicts any queued work for
that sync id from the scheduler.
.br
.B Why it exists:
reversing
.B sync add
must be atomic \- both the persisted SQLite row and the in-flight scheduler
queue have to be cleaned up together. Editing the store by hand would leave
orphaned queued operations running against a vanished sync root.
.br
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc sync remove " \fISYNC-ID\fR .
.I SYNC-ID
is a
.BR u64 ;
non-numeric values are rejected with
.BR InvalidRequest .
Passing a local path is
.B not
supported by the current Rust CLI \- resolve it to a numeric id first via
.BR "sync list" .
If the positional is omitted the CLI prompts for it.
.br
.B Preconditions:
daemon reachable; the
.I SYNC-ID
must correspond to an existing row.
.br
.B Output shape:
empty
.I result
on success, status
.BR Ok .
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync remove 42                                    # beginner
id=$(pcloudc \-\-json sync list \\
    | jq -r '.result.sync_roots[] | select(.local_path=="/home/me/Photos") | .sync_id')
pcloudc sync remove "$id"                                 # scripted
pcloudc \-\-json sync remove 9999                          # troubleshooting (NotFound)
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR InvalidRequest " - non-numeric id;"
.BR NotFound " - sync id absent;"
.BR Unavailable " - daemon down."
.br
.B Related:
.BR "sync list" ", " "sync add" .
.TP
.B sync change-type \fISYNC-ID\fR \fIFLAVOR\fR
.RB "(aliases: " "sync set-type" ", " "sync retype" ", " sync-change-type )
.B Purpose:
flip the direction of an existing sync root in-place (bilateral \(->
mirror, mirror \(-> bilateral, etc.) without unregistering and re-adding
the pairing.
.br
.B Why it exists:
the persistent
.BR sync_id ,
remote folder binding, and crash-safe staging context are expensive to
reconstruct. Switching direction at runtime preserves them while
invalidating only the already-queued work whose direction no longer
matches the new plan. Mirrors C
.BR psync_change_synctype .
.br
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc sync change-type " \fISYNC-ID\fR " " \fIFLAVOR\fR .
.RS
.TP 4
.I SYNC-ID
.BR u64 ;
non-numeric values are rejected with
.BR InvalidRequest .
Resolve a local path to an id via
.BR "sync list" .
.TP 4
.I FLAVOR
one of the 9 aliases from the
.B Sync flavors
subsection above. Unknown aliases are rejected with
.BR InvalidRequest ;
the error lists all 9 aliases.
.RE
Both positionals are required. If omitted the CLI falls back to an
interactive prompt.
.br
.B Preconditions:
daemon reachable, authenticated session, existing
.IR SYNC-ID .
.br
.B Output shape:
a human-readable
.I message
reporting the before/after labels (
.BR full ", " "download-only" ", " "upload-only" ").
No-op (same flavor) returns
.B Ok
with a "already has sync type X" message.
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync change-type 7 mirror                         # beginner
pcloudc sync change-type 7 bilateral                      # flip back
pcloudc sync-change-type 42 backup                        # canonical token
                                                          # (see caveat above)
id=$(pcloudc \-\-json sync list \\
    | jq -r '.result.sync_roots[] | select(.local_path=="/home/me/Docs") | .sync_id')
pcloudc sync change-type "$id" mirror                     # scripted
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR InvalidRequest " - non-numeric id, unknown FLAVOR, or missing positional;"
.BR Conflict " - sync id does not exist;"
.BR InternalError " - persistence failure (rolled back in memory);"
.BR Unavailable " - daemon down."
.br
.B Related:
.BR "sync list" ", " "sync add" ", " "snapshot create" .
.TP
.BR "sync pause" ", " pause
.B Purpose:
daemon-wide pause of all sync activity.
.br
.B Why it exists:
the current IPC surface exposes only a daemon-wide pause (matching legacy C
behaviour). Use this as a quick halt before a network switch, on metered
Wi-Fi, or during battery saving \- without tearing the sync graph down. A
.I per-root
pause is not exposed on the CLI today.
.br
.B Syntax:
.B pcloudc sync pause
(equivalent to the top-level
.B pcloudc pause
alias; no arguments).
.br
.B Preconditions:
daemon reachable. Idempotent on an already-paused daemon.
.br
.B Output shape:
status
.B Ok
with empty result. Subsequent
.B sync list
rows surface
.BR "paused = true" .
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync pause                                        # beginner
pcloudc \-\-json sync pause | jq '.status'                 # scripted
pcloudc \-q sync pause && nmcli networking off            # troubleshooting
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR Unavailable " - daemon not running."
.br
.B Related:
.BR "sync resume" ", " pending ", " status .
.TP
.BR "sync resume" ", " resume
.B Purpose:
undo the daemon-wide pause and let the scheduler drain queued work.
.br
.B Why it exists:
symmetric counterpart to
.BR "sync pause" .
.br
.B Syntax:
.B pcloudc sync resume
(no arguments).
.br
.B Preconditions:
daemon reachable.
.br
.B Output shape:
status
.B Ok ;
the scheduler resumes immediately.
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync resume                                       # beginner
pcloudc \-\-json sync resume                               # scripted
pcloudc sync resume && pcloudc pending                    # troubleshooting
.fi
.RE
.B Related:
.BR "sync pause" ", " pending .
.TP
.BR "sync localscan" ", " localscan ", " sync-localscan ", " run-localscan
.B Purpose:
force an immediate local-filesystem rescan across every sync root. Mirrors
the C
.B psync_run_localscan
entry point.
.br
.B Why it exists:
editors that rewrite files via rename-then-swap, bind-mount directories,
and network filesystems can all deliver changes that bypass
.BR inotify ,
so the daemon may miss events until the next periodic scan. This
subcommand wakes the scheduler and forces a tree walk right now.
.br
.B Syntax:
.B pcloudc sync localscan
(no arguments, no flags).
.br
.B Preconditions:
daemon reachable; at least one sync root is useful but not required (with
zero roots the scheduler cycles immediately with no work).
.br
.B Output shape:
status
.B Ok
on dispatch; the actual scan is asynchronous. Follow up with
.B pending
to observe the resulting work items.
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync localscan                                    # beginner
pcloudc \-q sync localscan && sleep 2 && pcloudc pending   # scripted
pcloudc \-\-json sync localscan | jq '.status'             # troubleshooting
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR Unavailable " - daemon not running."
.br
.B Related:
.BR pending ", " status ", " "fs status" .
.TP
.BI "sync suggest " "[ PATH ] [ \-\-max " N ]
.RB "(single\-token: " sync-suggest )
.B Purpose:
scan the local filesystem under
.I PATH
(default: home directory) and return a list of candidate folders that could
be added as sync roots. Mirrors C
.BR psync_get_sync_suggestions .
.br
.B Why it exists:
onboarding scripts can propose sync roots without hard-coding paths. The
heuristic walks the top-level directory tree and scores folders by size,
recency, and absence of existing sync root overlap.
.br
.B Flags:
.RS
.TP 4
.I PATH
positional base path. Optional. Defaults to the user's home directory.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max " N
hard cap on the number of suggestions returned. No cap by default.
.RE
.B Output shape:
JSON array of suggestion objects in
.IR message .
Each entry contains at minimum
.B path
and a classification label.
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync suggest ~/work \-\-max 5           # beginner
pcloudc \-\-json sync suggest | jq '.[].path'   # scripted
.fi
.RE
.B Related:
.BR "sync add" ", " "sync is-syncable" .
.TP
.BI "sync is-syncable " PATH
.RB "(single\-token: " sync-is-syncable ", alias: " "sync syncable" )
.B Purpose:
classify whether
.I PATH
can be added as a sync root without conflicting with already-registered roots.
Returns
.B Ok
if the path is eligible,
.B Conflict
if it duplicates or nests inside an existing root. Mirrors C
.BR psync_is_folder_syncable .
.br
.B Syntax:
.BI "pcloudc sync is-syncable " PATH
.RS
.TP 4
.I PATH
absolute local path to classify. Required; falls back to an interactive prompt if omitted.
.RE
.B Output shape:
status
.B Ok
(eligible) or
.B Conflict
(already covered).
.br
.B Exit codes:
.B 0
\(em eligible;
.B 7 Conflict
\(em already covered by another root.
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync is-syncable ~/Documents     # beginner: exit 0 \(-> safe
pcloudc sync is-syncable ~/Documents     # exit 7 \(-> already a root
if pcloudc \-q sync is-syncable "$dir"; then pcloudc sync add "$dir" /Drive; fi
.fi
.RE
.B Related:
.BR "sync suggest" ", " "sync add" .
.SS Mounted drive
.PP
.B Honest scope.
The mounted-drive runtime in this release is a
.B pre-alpha Linux-only scaffold
(tracked under bd-1du.4). The CLI surface below is wired, the daemon's
mount/unmount state machine is real, and a default-to-null FUSE adapter is
loaded at mount time, but the end-to-end FUSE read/write path is not yet at
parity with the legacy C client. macOS, Windows, and BSD do
.B not
yet have real FUSE/WinFSP wiring. Treat this subsection as a contract for
how the flags behave today rather than a promise about filesystem feature
coverage. On non-Linux hosts the mount request is accepted by the CLI but
the daemon returns
.BR Unavailable .
.PP
.B Concept primer \- mount vs sync.
.B mount
exposes the remote drive as a FUSE filesystem: directory entries and file
metadata come from the server on demand, reads fetch bytes through a
write-back-capable page cache, and nothing is persisted into a local sync
graph. It is the opposite of
.BR "sync add" ,
which
.I copies
files to disk and reconciles both sides. The two are complementary, not
redundant; see the
.B Sync folders
concept primer above.
.TP
.B mount \fR[\fIMOUNTPOINT\fR] \fR[\-m \fIPATH\fR] \fR[\-O \fIOPTS\fR] \fR[\-\-cache-size \fIGB\fR]
.B Purpose:
project the remote pCloud drive into a local FUSE mount point so that any
process on the host can read files from it on demand.
.br
.B Why it exists:
users who do not want a full local mirror (think: multi-terabyte accounts
on a 256 GB laptop) still need a predictable way to open, read, and stream
files from pCloud via standard POSIX calls. Unlike
.BR "sync add" ,
.B mount
does not persist local copies; it is also the only surface that accepts
the one-shot FUSE override flags below.
.br
.B Syntax + positionals + flags:
.RS
.TP 4
.I MOUNTPOINT
optional positional, absolute local path. Type: filesystem path.
Validation: must exist and be empty; ownership and permissions are
re-checked daemon-side (no
.B allow_other
by default).
.TP 4
.BI "\-m, \-\-mountpoint " PATH
one-shot override that takes precedence over the positional
.I MOUNTPOINT
for this invocation only. Same validation. Honoured by the daemon.
.TP 4
.BI "\-O, \-\-fuse-opts " OPTS
comma-separated FUSE option string (for example
.BR allow_other ", " default_permissions ).
.B Honest scope:
the CLI parses and forwards this flag to the daemon, and the request
payload carries it, but the runtime does
.B not
yet thread the value into the mount-time FUSE argv. Landing that plumbing
is tracked in the bead tracker under the filesystem parity epic
(bd-1du.4). For the current release the daemon logs the override and
consults its configured defaults.
.TP 4
.BI "\-\-cache-size " GB
one-shot write-back cache cap, in gigabytes. Type:
.BR u64 .
Validation: non-negative integer.
.B Honest scope:
same status as
.B \-\-fuse-opts
\- parsed and forwarded but not yet honoured at mount construction (see
bd-1du.4). The companion
.B PCLOUD_CACHE_SIZE_GB
environment variable
.I is
honoured at shim-factory construction and remains the supported way to
size the page cache today.
.RE
.B Preconditions:
authenticated session (mount without auth falls back to a null FUSE adapter
that returns
.B ENOSYS
\- a debugging aid, not a usable mount); Linux host;
.B fusermount
available in
.BR PATH ;
the user is in the
.B fuse
group or has the equivalent capability.
.br
.B Mountpoint resolution order:
explicit
.B \-m
flag wins first, then the positional
.IR MOUNTPOINT ,
then the
.B PCLOUD_DEFAULT_MOUNTPOINT
environment variable, and finally an interactive prompt when none of those
are set.
.br
.B Output shape:
status
.B Ok
once the FUSE session binds; the response carries the effective
.I mountpoint
path. Errors surface the mountpoint validator's reason string (non-empty
directory, bad permissions, already mounted, etc.).
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc mount ~/pCloudDrive                               # beginner
pcloudc \-\-json mount \-m /mnt/pcloud                      # scripted
PCLOUD_DEFAULT_MOUNTPOINT=/mnt/pcloud pcloudc mount       # env fallback
pcloudc mount \-\-cache-size 5 \-O allow_other             # flags (parse-only today)
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR InvalidRequest " - mountpoint missing, not empty, or not owned by invoker;"
.BR Unavailable " - non-Linux host or FUSE library unavailable;"
.BR Unauthorized " - no session (daemon serves the null adapter instead);"
.BR Conflict " - another pCloud mount already holds this path (use " unmount ")."
.br
.B Related:
.BR unmount ", " "fs status" ", " "sync add" .
.TP
.BR unmount ", " umount
.B Purpose:
release the active pCloud FUSE mount point.
.br
.B Why it exists:
the mount runs inside the daemon process; a plain
.B fusermount \-u
outside the daemon would leave its internal bookkeeping stale. The
.B unmount
subcommand runs the drain hook first so in-flight writes are flushed, then
tears the FUSE session down cleanly.
.br
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc unmount " \fR[\fIMOUNTPOINT\fR].
The positional
.I MOUNTPOINT
is consulted only when routing to
.I force
unmount (see the envvar below); the graceful path takes no argument and
acts on whatever the daemon currently has bound.
.br
.B Force-unmount envvar:
when
.B PCLOUD_FORCE_UMOUNT
is set to a truthy token
.RB ( 1 ", " true ", " yes ", " on ,
case-insensitive) the CLI promotes a bare
.B pcloudc unmount
to a forced request (equivalent to
.BR "pcloudc mount \-\-force\-umount " \fIPATH\fR )
by dispatching
.B MountForceUnmount
internally. The mount path is resolved in this order: positional argument,
.BR PCLOUD_DEFAULT_MOUNTPOINT ,
and when neither is set the CLI falls back to the graceful path so the
daemon acts on its own bookkeeping. This is the recommended recovery for
.I orphan
mounts left behind by a crashed previous daemon.
.br
.B Preconditions:
daemon reachable; for the forced path the target
.I PATH
must be a current or stale FUSE entry readable by the invoking user.
.br
.B Output shape:
status
.B Ok
once the mount releases. Errors surface the kernel's
.B umount(2)
errno (for example
.BR EBUSY ).
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc unmount                                           # beginner, graceful
PCLOUD_FORCE_UMOUNT=1 pcloudc unmount                     # force via envvar + default
PCLOUD_FORCE_UMOUNT=yes pcloudc unmount /home/alice/pCloudDrive   # force + explicit path
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR Unavailable " - daemon not running;"
.BR NotFound " - no mount bound;"
.BR Conflict " - open file handles on the mountpoint (close them, or set " PCLOUD_FORCE_UMOUNT=1 ")."
.br
.B Related:
.BR mount ", " "fs status" .
.SS fs family
.PP
.B Concept primer \- fs operates on local paths.
The
.B fs
subcommands query the
.I local
filesystem's relationship to the sync engine. They do
.B not
operate on remote pCloud paths \- that responsibility belongs to the
.B folder
subcommands and to the public-link commands. The only
.B fs
subcommand exposed today is
.B fs status .
Other members of the family
.RB ( "fs ls" ", " "fs cp" ", " "fs mv" ", " "fs rm" ", " "fs mkdir" ", " "fs read" ", " "fs write" )
are
.B not yet implemented
in the Rust CLI; for remote-side id/metadata work use the
.B folder
subcommands below.
.TP
.B fs status \fILOCAL-PATH\fR
.RB "(aliases: " fs-status ", " filesystem-status )
.B Purpose:
classify a local path against the daemon's sync graph and engine state, so
scripts can decide whether a given file is up to date, in progress, outside
any sync root, or unusable. Mirrors the C
.B psync_filesystem_status
entry point.
.br
.B Why it exists:
a shell script that has just written a file into a sync folder needs a
stable way to answer "is this file safely on the server yet?" without
parsing
.B pending
and cross-referencing sync ids manually.
.B fs status
encapsulates that decision.
.br
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc fs status " \fILOCAL-PATH\fR .
.RS
.TP 4
.I LOCAL-PATH
absolute local filesystem path. Type: string. Validation: must not be
empty; relative paths are accepted by the CLI but the classifier uses the
path verbatim, so prefer absolute forms.
.RE
.B Preconditions:
daemon reachable. Works even when no sync roots are configured (returns
.B NOSYNC
for every path).
.br
.B Output shape \- field semantics:
the daemon returns a single token at
.IR result.message :
.RS
.TP 10
.B INSYNC
the path is under a sync root and the engine has no queued or errored work
for that sync id \- treat this as "healthy".
.TP 10
.B INPROG
the path is under a sync root and the engine still has queued operations
for that sync id \- transfers are in progress; re-check after
.BR pending .
.TP 10
.B NOSYNC
the path is not under any configured sync root \- from the daemon's point
of view it is not tracked. This is
.B not
an error, just "outside the sync graph".
.TP 10
.B INVSYNC
the path is under a sync root but that sync id has an errored queued
operation (conflict), which the classifier treats as "degraded" \- the user
must investigate and resolve before transfers resume.
.RE
In scripting terms, "healthy" is
.BR INSYNC ;
"degraded" is
.B INVSYNC
or a persistent
.BR INPROG .
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc fs status ~/Documents/report.pdf                  # beginner
test "$(pcloudc \-\-json fs status /srv/data/out.bin | jq -r .message)" = INSYNC \\
    && echo uploaded                                       # scripted
pcloudc \-\-json fs status /home/me/Photos | jq .         # troubleshooting
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR InvalidRequest " - empty path;"
.BR Unavailable " - daemon not running."
.br
.B Related:
.BR "sync list" ", " "sync localscan" ", " pending .
.SS Public links
.TP
.BR "create-file-link " \fIREMOTE-FILE\fR
.B When to use this:
share a single file by URL.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc create-file-link /Reports/2026/Q1.pdf
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BR "create-folder-link " \fIREMOTE-FOLDER\fR
.B When to use this:
share a whole folder by URL (read\-only by default).
.RS
.nf
pcloudc create-folder-link /Public/Brochures
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BR "create-upload-link " \fIREMOTE-FOLDER\fR
.B When to use this:
let someone upload into a folder you own without them needing an account.
.TP
.BR "create-tree-link "
.B When to use this:
publish a custom tree of specific files and folders as one link (use
.BR "folder-id"
to resolve ids).
.TP
.BR list-links ", " list-public-links
.B When to use this:
list every public link on the account.
.TP
.B list-upload-links
.B When to use this:
list just the upload\-only links.
.TP
.BR "show-link " \fICODE\fR
.B When to use this:
inspect a single link (expiry, password, access list, download stats).
.TP
.BR "delete-link " \fILINK-ID-OR-CODE\fR ", " "delete-upload-link " \fILINK-ID\fR
.B When to use this:
revoke a link.
.B delete-link
accepts either a numeric link id or the short share code printed by
.B list-links
/
.BR create-file-link .
When a non-numeric argument is given, the daemon resolves the code to
an id by scanning the current account's public links.
.B delete-upload-link
still requires the numeric id (the upload-link list already surfaces it).
.TP
.BR "change-link-expire " \fILINK-ID\fR " [\fIEXPIRE\fR]"
.B When to use this:
set or clear the expiry on an existing link. The
.I EXPIRE
argument accepts both a UNIX seconds timestamp and an ISO-8601 civil
date (\fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR, interpreted as midnight UTC). Omit it, or pass
.B clear
(equivalently
.BR none ),
to remove the expiry.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc change-link-expire 42 1798675200     # unix-ts form
pcloudc change-link-expire 42 2026-12-31     # ISO-8601 date form
pcloudc change-link-expire 42                # clear the expiry
pcloudc change-link-expire 42 clear          # clear the expiry
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BR "change-link-password " \fICODE\fR " [\fIPASSWORD\fR]"
.B When to use this:
set or remove a password on a link.
.TP
.BR "change-link-upload " \fICODE\fR " \fIeveryone|chosen|off\fR"
.B When to use this:
decide who (if anyone) may upload into a folder link.
.TP
.BR list-link-access ", " add-link-access ", " remove-link-access
.B When to use this:
manage the chosen\-users list when upload policy is
.BR chosen .
.TP
.BR "publink send " \fICODE\fR " \-\-to \fIEMAIL\fR \-\-message \fIMSG\fR"
.RB "(alias: " publink-send ", " send-publink )
.B When to use this:
email a public link from the daemon.
.TP
.BR list-bookmarks ", " "remove-bookmark " \fIID\fR ", " "change-bookmark " \fIID\fR
.B When to use this:
manage pinned links in your pCloud bookmarks.
.SS Sharing with people
.TP
.BR "share-folder " \fIREMOTE-FOLDER\fR
.B When to use this:
invite a contact to a folder with a chosen permission set.
.TP
.BR list-incoming-shares ", " list-outgoing-shares
.B When to use this:
audit who is sharing folders with you and who you are sharing with.
.TP
.BR list-incoming-share-requests ", " list-outgoing-share-requests
.B When to use this:
see pending invitations.
.TP
.BR accept-share-request ", " decline-share-request ", " cancel-share-request
.B When to use this:
respond to an invitation you received, or retract one you sent.
.TP
.BR modify-share ", " remove-share
.B When to use this:
change a collaborator's permissions or revoke their access.
.TP
.BR account-stopshare ", " account-modifyshare ", " account-teamshare
.B When to use this:
business\- and team\-account share operations.
.TP
.BR list-contacts ", " list-myteams
.B When to use this:
see who is available to share with.
.SS Crypto folder
.TP
.B crypto start
.RB "(alias: " unlock-crypto )
.B When to use this:
unlock the crypto folder for this session. You are prompted for the crypto
password. Most clients also support
.BR "\-\-crypto, \-c "
on
.B login
to unlock at sign\-in time.
.TP
.B crypto stop
.RB "(alias: " lock-crypto )
.B When to use this:
re\-lock the crypto folder immediately.
.TP
.B crypto status
.RB "(alias: " crypto-status )
.B When to use this:
see whether crypto is locked, unlocked, or uninitialized.
.TP
.B crypto reset
.RB "(single\-token: " crypto-reset )
.B When to use this:
wipe the local crypto fingerprint and folder registry. Recovery-only operation \(em
the server-side encrypted data is not deleted. After a reset, the next
.B crypto start
re-downloads and re-verifies the fingerprint from the server.
.TP
.B crypto hint
.RB "(single\-token: " crypto-hint )
.B When to use this:
fetch the passphrase hint stored at first-time crypto setup. The hint is
never the passphrase itself; it is a free-text reminder chosen by the user.
Requires crypto to be set up, but does not require it to be unlocked.
.TP
.B crypto priv-key-flags
.RB "(single\-token: " crypto-priv-key-flags )
.B When to use this:
return the current
.B crypto_private_flags
row as a decimal integer. Encodes metadata such as whether a temporary
password is set
.RB ( PSYNC_CRYPTO_FLAG_TEMP_PASS ).
Mirrors C
.BR psync_crypto_priv_key_flags .
.TP
.B crypto send-change-private
.RB "(single\-token: " crypto-send-change-private ", aliases: " "crypto send-change" )
.B When to use this:
request a server-side one-time confirmation code that authorises a
subsequent
.B crypto change-password
call. The code is delivered out-of-band (e.g. email). Run this first,
then run
.B crypto change-password
once you have the code. Mirrors C
.BR psync_crypto_crypto_send_change_user_private .
.TP
.B "crypto change-password " \fR[\fIOLD\fR [\fINEW\fR [\fIHINT\fR [\fICODE\fR]]]]
.RB "(single\-token: " crypto-change-password )
.B When to use this:
rotate the crypto passphrase. The shell may be locked \(em the old password
is verified before any change is applied. Prompts for: old passphrase, new
passphrase, hint, and the server confirmation code from
.BR "crypto send-change-private" .
Mirrors C
.BR psync_crypto_change_crypto_pass .
Transit-only secrets; no passphrase is written to disk.
.RS
.TP 4
.I OLD
Current crypto passphrase. Prompted if omitted.
.TP
.I NEW
Replacement crypto passphrase. Prompted if omitted.
.TP
.I HINT
Free-text hint stored with the new passphrase (positional arg 3).
.TP
.I CODE
Server-side confirmation code from
.B crypto send-change-private
(positional arg 4).
.RE
.TP
.B "crypto change-password-unlocked " \fR[\fINEW\fR [\fIHINT\fR [\fICODE\fR]]]
.RB "(single\-token: " crypto-change-password-unlocked )
.B When to use this:
same as
.B crypto change-password
but valid only when the crypto shell is already unlocked. The old passphrase
is not required. Mirrors C
.BR psync_crypto_change_crypto_pass_unlocked .
.SS Files, folders, and transfers
.PP
.B Concept primer \- folder operates on remote paths.
The
.B folder
subcommands act on absolute paths inside your pCloud-drive namespace (for
example
.BR /Documents/Reports ),
not on local filesystem paths. They are the remote-side counterpart to the
.B fs status
classifier above.
All four require an authenticated session
.RB ( "pcloudc login" ).
.TP
.B folder create \fIPATH\fR
.RB "(alias: " folder-create )
.B Purpose:
create a remote folder by absolute pCloud-drive path. Mirrors the C
.B psync_create_remote_folder_by_path
entry point.
.br
.B Why it exists:
scripts that later call
.B sync add
or
.B create-folder-link
need a reliable way to materialise the target folder up-front without
knowing its numeric parent id. This subcommand takes a human-readable
absolute path and dispatches to the IPC variant with
.IR parent_folder_id = None .
.br
.B Syntax + positional:
.BR "pcloudc folder create " \fIPATH\fR .
.RS
.TP 4
.I PATH
absolute pCloud-drive path. Type: string. Validation: must start with
.BR / ;
the daemon creates intermediate folders as needed when the backend supports
it.
.RE
.B Preconditions:
authenticated session.
.br
.B Output shape:
.BR result.folder_id " and " result.path
on success.
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc folder create /Documents/Reports                  # beginner
pcloudc \-\-json folder create /Backups/2026 | jq '.result.folder_id'  # scripted
pcloudc folder create Documents                           # troubleshooting: rejected, not absolute
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR InvalidRequest " - missing path or non-absolute;"
.BR Conflict " - folder already exists;"
.BR Unauthorized " - not logged in."
.br
.B Related:
.BR "folder id" ", " "sync add" ", " "create-folder-link" .
.TP
.B folder id \fIPATH\fR
.RB "(alias: " folder-id )
.B Purpose:
resolve an absolute pCloud-drive path to its numeric folder id. Mirrors C
.BR psync_get_fsfolderid_by_path .
.br
.B Why it exists:
many other surfaces (including
.BR create-tree-link ,
account-team-share, and several SDK helpers) take a numeric
.I folder_id
rather than a path; this is the canonical resolver.
.br
.B Syntax + positional:
.BR "pcloudc folder id " \fIPATH\fR .
.I PATH
is an absolute pCloud-drive path; rejected when missing or relative.
.br
.B Preconditions:
authenticated session; the path must already exist.
.br
.B Output shape:
.I result.folder_id
as a
.BR u64 .
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc folder id /Documents                              # beginner
id=$(pcloudc \-\-json folder id /Public | jq -r '.result.folder_id')   # scripted
pcloudc folder id /does/not/exist                         # troubleshooting: NotFound
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR NotFound " - path does not exist;"
.BR InvalidRequest " - missing or non-absolute path."
.br
.B Related:
.BR "folder flags" ", " "folder owner" ", " "folder create" ", " "create-tree-link" .
.TP
.B folder flags \fIPATH\fR
.B Purpose:
read folder capability flags for an absolute pCloud-drive path. Mirrors C
.BR psync_get_fsfolderflags_by_id .
.br
.B Why it exists:
before calling a destructive or permission-dependent surface (for example,
sharing or creating a sub-folder link) you may need to confirm that the
folder is writeable, shareable, or owned by the current account.
.br
.B Syntax + positional:
.BR "pcloudc folder flags " \fIPATH\fR .
.I PATH
is an absolute pCloud-drive path.
.br
.B Preconditions:
authenticated session; path must exist.
.br
.B Output shape:
.I result.flags
is a numeric bitmask; see the pCloud API docs for individual bit meanings.
Scripts typically mask with
.BR 0x08 " (writeable) or " 0x20 " (shareable)."
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc folder flags /Documents                           # beginner
pcloudc \-\-json folder flags /Shared | jq '.result.flags'  # scripted
pcloudc folder flags /Trash                               # troubleshooting
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR NotFound ", " InvalidRequest ", " Unauthorized .
.br
.B Related:
.BR "folder id" ", " "folder owner" ", " "shares" .
.TP
.B folder owner \fIPATH\fR
.B Purpose:
read the owner user id of a remote folder. Mirrors C
.BR psync_get_folder_ownerid .
.br
.B Why it exists:
on shared folders (incoming shares, team-share folders) you need to know
who owns the folder before deciding whether to request a permission change
or detach.
.br
.B Syntax + positional:
.BR "pcloudc folder owner " \fIPATH\fR .
.I PATH
is an absolute pCloud-drive path.
.br
.B Preconditions:
authenticated session; path must exist.
.br
.B Output shape:
.I result.owner_user_id
as a
.BR u64 .
.br
.B Examples:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc folder owner /Documents                           # beginner
pcloudc \-\-json folder owner /SharedWithMe/Team \\
    | jq '.result.owner_user_id'                          # scripted
pcloudc folder owner /missing                             # troubleshooting: NotFound
.fi
.RE
.B Failure modes:
.BR NotFound ", " InvalidRequest ", " Unauthorized .
.br
.B Related:
.BR "folder id" ", " "folder flags" ", " "shares list-incoming" .
.TP
.B pending
.B When to use this:
show queued uploads and downloads.
.TP
.B userinfo
.B Purpose:
fetch the authenticated user's profile, plan, and quota.
.B Why it exists:
the most common "is my login actually working and is this the right
account?" smoke test, and the canonical source for scripts that need quota
or plan metadata.
.B Syntax:
.BR "pcloudc [\-\-json] userinfo [FIELDS...]" .
No flags of its own. Trailing positional arguments act as a native field
selector (equivalent to repeating
.B \-\-field
for each one).
.B Authentication requirements:
requires an active session.
.B Output shape (top\-level JSON keys):
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.I user_id
\(em numeric pCloud user id. Stable across sessions.
.IP \(bu 2
.I email
\(em account email address.
.IP \(bu 2
.I emailverified
\(em boolean; true once the user has verified their address.
.IP \(bu 2
.I quota
\(em total bytes allocated on the plan.
.IP \(bu 2
.I usedquota
\(em bytes currently consumed.
.IP \(bu 2
.I premium
\(em boolean; true for paid plans.
.IP \(bu 2
.I premiumexpires
\(em UNIX seconds until paid plan expires (only when
.IR premium: true ).
.IP \(bu 2
.I plan
\(em plan identifier string.
.RE
.B Top 5 most useful fields for scripts:
.IR quota ", "
.IR usedquota ", "
.IR premium ", "
.IR emailverified ", "
.IR email .
.RS
.nf
# 1) Beginner:
pcloudc userinfo

# 2) Scripted \(em just the two quota numbers (native field selector):
pcloudc userinfo quota usedquota \-\-json

# 3) Scripted \(em with a computed percentage via jq:
pcloudc \-\-json userinfo \\
    | jq '(.usedquota / .quota) * 100 | floor'

# 4) Troubleshooting \(em verify the session is pointed at the right account:
pcloudc \-\-json userinfo email user_id emailverified
.fi
.RE
.B Common failure modes:
.IR "not authenticated"
(run
.BR login ),
.IR "daemon unreachable"
(run
.B start
/ check the socket),
.IR "stale session"
(token revoked server\-side \(em re\-run
.BR login ).
.B Related:
.BR session-status ", "
.BR status ", "
.BR login .
.SS Notifications, audit, and diagnostics
.TP
.BR list-notifications ", " "notifications list" ", " "notifications mark-read " \fIID\fR
.B When to use this:
read, and silence, account notifications.
.TP
.BR "audit verify" ", " audit-verify
.B When to use this:
verify the local audit log chain.
.TP
.B session status
.RB "(alias: " session-status )
.B When to use this:
as above; see
.BR session-status .
.SS Snapshots (disaster recovery) \(em see \fBSNAPSHOTS\fR section
.PP
The
.B snapshot
family (tar \(-> zstd \(-> SHA3\-256 \(-> sidecar manifest, optional GPG
wrap) is documented in its own
.B SNAPSHOTS
section later in this page. Quick map:
.B snapshot
(bare) \(== default
.BR "snapshot create" ;
.BR "snapshot create" " writes " <archive>.tar.zst " + " <archive>.tar.zst.manifest.json ;
.BR "snapshot verify" " re\-hashes the archive against its manifest;"
.BR "snapshot restore" " unpacks a verified archive over the state dir;"
.BR "snapshot prune" " applies a retention policy to an artifact dir."
.PP
The legacy
.B backup snapshot-*
forms still parse but emit a stderr deprecation warning and route to the
new pipeline.
.SS Integrity sweeper
The integrity sweeper is a background scrub worker that periodically
re-verifies local files against the daemon's recorded expectations. It
is opt-in via
.BR pcloud.conf (5)
under
.BR [features.integrity_sweeper] .
See the
.B "Verifying local-vs-server integrity on a schedule"
playbook in the operations runbook.
.TP
.B integrity status
.B When to use this:
inspect the sweeper: enabled flag, last-run timestamp, current counters
per result kind, dropped-audit count, and the resolved skip-list path.
Default subcommand when
.B integrity
is invoked without an argument.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc integrity status
pcloudc \-\-json integrity status
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B integrity run-once
.B When to use this:
trigger a single sweep and print the delta report. Use from cron or an
operator shell to verify-on-demand without enabling the background
schedule. Honours the configured rate limit and skip list.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc integrity run-once
pcloudc \-\-json integrity run-once
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B integrity skip \fIPATH\fR
.B When to use this:
append
.I PATH
to the on-disk skip list and reload it. Subsequent sweeps will not
verify that path.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc integrity skip ~/pCloud/build-artifacts
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Result kinds
reported by
.B integrity status
and
.B integrity run-once :
.TP
.B Ok
File content matched the recorded expectation. Not audited (silent).
.TP
.B Mismatch
Size, mtime, or content hash diverged from the daemon's record. Emits
an
.B integrity.mismatch
audit event with a path-HMAC (never the cleartext path). Investigate
with
.B pcloudc audit verify
and reconcile by hand.
.TP
.B LocalMissing
The daemon expected the file locally but it is gone (deleted out of
band, quarantined by a scanner, rename race). Audited.
.TP
.B RemoteMissing
The server-side record the sweeper cross-checked against has vanished
(removed remotely while the local copy remains). Audited.
.TP
.B Throttled
The token bucket was empty when this file came up; it was skipped this
round and will be retried next tick. Only the
.I throttled
counter is bumped; no audit row is written.
.TP
.B FetchFailed
The sweeper could not fetch the server-side checksum (transient network
or API failure). Audited so persistent fetch outages are visible.
.SS Integrity and revision history
.TP
.B verify \fIPATH\fR \fR[\fB\-\-recursive\fR] \fR[\fB\-\-fix\fR] \fR[\fB\-\-yes\fR]
.B When to use this:
reconcile a sync root (or a single file) against server state after a crash,
disk swap, or suspected local tamper. Walks the store, hashes each local
object, and compares against the remote hash. One line per object in a
stable taxonomy:
.RS
.nf
[OK]                                 <path>
[MISMATCH local=<hex> server=<hex>]  <path>
[MISSING_LOCAL]                      <path>
[MISSING_REMOTE]                     <path>
.fi
.PP
Options:
.BR \-\-recursive
(descend into every subfolder of
.IR PATH ),
.BR \-\-fix
(ask the daemon to re\-download
.B MISMATCH
and
.B MISSING_LOCAL
entries and to re\-upload
.B MISSING_REMOTE
entries; destructive, requires interactive confirmation or
.BR \-\-yes ),
.BR \-\-yes
(non\-interactive; pair with
.BR \-\-fix ).
.PP
With
.B \-\-json
the command streams one NDJSON object per record on stdout (one object per
line, not a wrapping array) so long walks can be piped into any
line-oriented consumer (log shipper, alerting pipeline, custom parser).
.PP
Exit codes:
.B 0 Ok
when every record is
.BR [OK] ;
.B 7 Conflict
as soon as any
.B [MISMATCH]
is observed (even with
.BR \-\-fix ,
so cron jobs notice);
.B 6 Unavailable
when the walk completes but one or more records were
.B MISSING_LOCAL
or
.B MISSING_REMOTE
and
.B \-\-fix
was not passed.
.PP
Examples:
.nf
pcloudc verify ~/Documents
pcloudc verify ~/Documents \-\-recursive
pcloudc \-\-json verify ~/Documents \-\-recursive    # NDJSON, one record/line
pcloudc verify ~/Photos \-\-recursive \-\-fix \-\-yes
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B log \fIPATH\fR \fR[\fB\-\-limit \fIN\fR]
.B When to use this:
list prior revisions of a single remote file.
.B Status:
scaffolded. The CLI parses the command and the daemon dispatches
.B Method::FileHistory ;
the backend currently returns
.B 6 Unavailable
because pCloud's
.B listrevisions
endpoint is undocumented and awaiting public approval. Tracked under
.BR bd-1du.10 .
.RS
.nf
pcloudc log /Documents/Plan.md
pcloudc log /Documents/Plan.md \-\-limit 20
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B diff \fIPATH\fR \fIREV_A\fR \fIREV_B\fR
.B When to use this:
compare two revision ids returned by
.BR log .
Same
.B 6 Unavailable
gating as
.B log
until the upstream endpoint is approved.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc diff /Documents/Plan.md 101 104
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B restore \fIPATH\fR \fIREV\fR
.B When to use this:
replace the current remote object with a named prior revision.
.B Status:
stubbed. Same
.B 6 Unavailable
gating as
.B log
and
.BR diff ;
see
.BR bd-1du.10 .
.RS
.nf
pcloudc restore /Documents/Plan.md 101
.fi
.RE
.SS Migration and shell integration
.TP
.BR "migrate-from-c " \fR[\fIARGS\fR]
.RB "(alias: " migrate )
.B When to use this:
import state (sync roots, ignore lists, config hints) from the legacy C
client on first run. Shipped in the current release (R8); previously a GA
blocker. The assistant refuses to overwrite an existing Rust store unless
.B \-\-force\-overwrite
is given, copies legacy files rather than moving them, and redacts
secret\-bearing fields in all preview output.
.PP
.B Synopsis:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc migrate\-from\-c [\-\-from \fIPATH\fR] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-force\-overwrite]
.fi
.RE
Options:
.BR "\-\-from " \fIPATH\fR
(override the auto\-detected legacy C state directory; defaults to
.I ~/.pcloud
on Linux/BSD,
.I ~/Library/Application\ Support/pcloud
on macOS, and
.I %APPDATA%\epcloud\e
on Windows),
.BR "\-\-dry\-run "
(print the import plan and validation results with secret fields redacted;
makes no writes),
.BR "\-\-force\-overwrite "
(allow importing on top of an existing Rust
.I .pclouddb
/ state directory; refused by default as a safety guard).
.PP
.B Safety guarantees:
legacy files are copied, never moved or deleted; refuses to start if a
Rust
.I .pclouddb
already exists without
.BR \-\-force\-overwrite ;
no cleartext passwords are imported (users must re\-authenticate); all
preview output routes through the secret\-redaction filter.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc migrate\-from\-c \-\-dry\-run
pcloudc migrate\-from\-c \-\-from ~/.pcloud
pcloudc migrate\-from\-c \-\-force\-overwrite
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B completion
.B When to use this:
emit shell completion scripts (see the CLI's
.B \-\-help
for the current shell list).
.SS Plugins
The daemon ships four first\-party single\-user plugins. They are
linked statically into
.B pcloudd
(there is no dynamic loader); each is enabled by a config flag and
the master switch
.BR PCLOUD_PLUGINS_ENABLED=1 .
Plugins run in\-process as trusted code; the host enforces declared
capabilities at dispatch time but does not sandbox.
.TP
.B publink-expiry
Desktop notification before a public link expires. Requires only
.BR ObserveStatus .
Enable with
.B [plugins.publink_expiry] enabled = true
in
.BR pcloud.conf (5).
Use with
.B change-link-expire
and
.B list-links
to act on a warning.
.TP
.B autoheal
Reacts to file\-integrity mismatches by notifying, quarantining the
affected path, and eventually pausing the sync root on repeated
failures. Requires
.B ObserveStatus
and
.BR SyncControl ,
so also set
.BR PCLOUD_PLUGIN_ALLOW_SYNC_CONTROL=1 .
Enable with
.B [plugins.autoheal] enabled = true .
.TP
.B backup-schedule
In\-process cron that resumes sync roots on a cron or natural\-language
schedule. Requires
.B SyncControl
(so also set
.BR PCLOUD_PLUGIN_ALLOW_SYNC_CONTROL=1 ).
Manage entries with
.BR "pcloudc backup schedule" " " "{list|add|remove}" .
.TP
.B dlp
Pre\-upload scanner for obvious secret material (AWS keys, PEM
private keys, JWTs, high\-entropy blobs). Audit\-only by default.
Enable with
.B [plugins.dlp] enabled = true ;
flip
.B strict_mode = true
to enforce.
.PP
See the plugin docs under
.I docs/plugins/
in the source tree for full configuration, behaviour, and known
limits.
.\" =====================================================================
.\" =====================================================================
.SH COMMAND REFERENCE (LINK / SHARE / BACKUP / CRYPTO / INTEGRITY / VERIFY / HISTORY)
.PP
Deep per\-command reference for the link, share, backup, crypto,
integrity, verify, and file\-history families. Every command is mapped to
a concrete daemon request type in
.I crates/pcloud-cli/src/commands.rs
and a handler in
.IR crates/pcloud-daemon/src/runtime.rs .
Response envelopes have two wire fields:
.BR status
(one of
.BR Ok ", " Conflict ", " InvalidRequest ", " InternalError ", " Unavailable )
and
.BR message
(free text or a JSON blob documented per\-command).
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1. Public links (concept primer)
.PP
Public links are one\-time\-URL shares that do
.B not
require the recipient to have a pCloud account. Four flavours:
.BR "file\-link " ( create-file-link ),
.BR "folder\-link " ( create-folder-link ),
.BR "tree\-link " ( create-tree-link ),
and
.BR "upload\-link " ( create-upload-link ,
reverse direction: recipient uploads into your folder). Every link has
an optional
.BR expire ,
an optional
.BR password ,
and (folder/tree only) an upload policy
.RB ( everyone | chosen | off ).
Links are identified by two handles: a numeric
.I link_id
(used by change\-* commands) and a human
.I code
(last URL segment, used by
.BR show-link ).
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1.1 create-file-link \fIREMOTE-FILE\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Mint a shareable URL for a single remote file.
.TP
.B Why
Distribute one document without granting account access; the most common
"send me a read\-only copy" case.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc create\-file\-link <REMOTE-FILE>
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Authenticated session; remote path must resolve to a file the current
user owns or may share.
.TP
.B Output shape
Daemon returns
.B status=Ok
and embeds a JSON object as
.BR message :
.RS
.nf
{"id":<u64>,"code":"<str>","is_folder":false,"link":"https://..."}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc create\-file\-link /Reports/2026/Q1.pdf
pcloudc \-\-json create\-file\-link /Reports/2026/Q1.pdf
pcloudc create\-file\-link /Reports/2026/Q1.pdf | tee /tmp/link.json
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(empty path),
.B 5 Auth
(no session),
.B 4 Network
(API unreachable),
.B 7 Conflict
(permission denied).
.TP
.B Related
.BR create-folder-link ", " create-upload-link ", " show-link ", " delete-link ", " change-link-expire ", " change-link-password .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1.2 create-folder-link \fIREMOTE-FOLDER\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Mint a shareable URL for a whole folder (download\-only by default).
.TP
.B Why
Hand a client a browseable directory without creating a share invitation.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc create\-folder\-link <REMOTE-FOLDER>
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Authenticated session; path must be a folder. To let recipients upload,
follow with
.B change-link-upload
.RB ( everyone | chosen ).
.TP
.B Output shape
Same JSON blob as
.B create-file-link
with
.B is_folder
set to
.BR true :
.RS
.nf
{"id":<u64>,"code":"<str>","is_folder":true,"link":"https://..."}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc create\-folder\-link /Public/Brochures
pcloudc \-\-json create\-folder\-link /Marketing/Q2
LID=$(pcloudc \-\-json create\-folder\-link /Inbox | jq \-r '.message | fromjson | .id')
pcloudc change\-link\-upload "$LID" chosen
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(empty path),
.B 5 Auth ,
.B 7 Conflict
(path is a file; permission denied).
.TP
.B Related
.BR change-link-upload ", " list-links ", " show-link ", " delete-link .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1.3 list-links, list-public-links
.TP
.B Purpose
Enumerate every public link on the current account (file, folder, tree,
upload).
.TP
.B Why
Inventory, audit, or feed into scripts that rotate/expire stale links.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc list\-links
pcloudc list\-public\-links           # synonym
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
plus a JSON object as
.BR message :
.RS
.nf
{
  "count": <N>,
  "links": [
    {"id":<u64>,"code":"<str>","name":"<str>",
     "is_folder":<bool>,"is_upload":<bool>,"link":"<url>"}
  ]
}
.fi
.RE
Field tips:
.BR .links.0.id
is the numeric id required by change\-* commands;
.BR .links.0.code
is the URL short code (accepted by
.BR show-link
and by the code\-form of
.BR delete-link );
.BR .links.0.is_upload
separates upload\-links from download\-links.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc list\-links
pcloudc \-\-field 'links.0.id' list\-links
pcloudc \-\-field 'count' list\-links
pcloudc \-\-json list\-links | jq '.message | fromjson | .links[] | select(.is_upload)'
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 5 Auth ", " 4 Network ,
.B 8 Internal
(malformed server response).
.TP
.B Related
.BR list-upload-links ", " show-link ", " delete-link ", " change-link-expire .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1.4 show-link \fICODE\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Inspect a single public link: code, expiry, password status, access
list, and (folder/tree) first level of contents.
.TP
.B Why
Verify what you are about to share before handing the URL out, or debug
why a recipient cannot download.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc show\-link <CODE>
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
.I CODE
is the last URL segment (e.g.
.BR XKzA1B2C3 ),
not the full URL.
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with free\-text
.B message :
.RS
.nf
public link contents: code="<code>", count=<N>,
  {name="...", is_folder=<bool>, item_id=<u64>}, ...
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc show\-link XKzA1B2C3
pcloudc \-\-json show\-link XKzA1B2C3
pcloudc show\-link                       # interactive secret\-prompt
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(empty/unknown code),
.B 5 Auth ,
.B 7 Conflict
(code belongs to a different account).
.TP
.B Related
.BR list-links ", " delete-link ", " change-link-password .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1.5 delete-link \fILINK-ID-OR-CODE\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Revoke a public link immediately.
.TP
.B Why
Kill a leaked URL, retire an expired share, or clean up old links found
via
.BR list-links .
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc delete\-link <LINK-ID>     # numeric
pcloudc delete\-link <CODE>        # short code
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
If
.I LINK-ID-OR-CODE
parses as a
.B u64
it is sent as an id
.RB ( DeletePublicLink );
otherwise the daemon sends
.BR DeletePublicLinkByCode ,
which re\-runs
.B list-links
to resolve the code (one extra API round\-trip).
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with
.B message
=
.BR "public link deleted: id=<N>" .
.B status=InvalidRequest
with
.BR "public link code not found: <code>"
on unknown code.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc delete\-link 42
pcloudc delete\-link XKzA1B2C3
# Revoke every expired link in one shot:
pcloudc \-\-json list\-links \\
  | jq \-r '.message | fromjson | .links[] | select(.expire and (.expire < now)) | .id' \\
  | xargs \-r \-n1 pcloudc delete\-link
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest ", " 5 Auth ", " 7 Conflict .
.TP
.B Related
.BR list-links ", " show-link ", " delete-upload-link .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1.6 change-link-expire \fILINK-ID\fR \fR[\fIEXPIRE\fR]
.TP
.B Purpose
Set or clear the expiry timestamp on an existing public link.
.TP
.B Why
Tighten or relax a share window without re\-minting the URL.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc change\-link\-expire <LINK-ID> <UNIX-SECONDS>
pcloudc change\-link\-expire <LINK-ID> <YYYY-MM-DD>
pcloudc change\-link\-expire <LINK-ID>          # clear
pcloudc change\-link\-expire <LINK-ID> clear    # clear (explicit)
pcloudc change\-link\-expire <LINK-ID> none     # clear (alias)
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Numeric
.I LINK-ID
(from
.BR list-links ).
.I YYYY-MM-DD
is interpreted as midnight UTC of that civil date.
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with
.B message
=
.BR "public link expire updated: id=<N>, expire=<ts>"
or
.BR "public link expire cleared: id=<N>" .
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc change\-link\-expire 42 1798675200
pcloudc change\-link\-expire 42 2026-12-31
pcloudc change\-link\-expire 42
pcloudc change\-link\-expire 42 clear
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(non\-numeric id; unparseable date),
.B 5 Auth ,
.B 7 Conflict .
.TP
.B Related
.BR list-links ", " change-link-password ", " change-link-upload .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1.7 change-link-password \fILINK-ID\fR \fR[\fIPASSWORD\fR]
.TP
.B Purpose
Gate an existing link behind a password, or remove the existing password.
.TP
.B Why
Add an out\-of\-band PSK when a link is being forwarded outside the
intended audience.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc change\-link\-password <LINK-ID> <PASSWORD>
pcloudc change\-link\-password <LINK-ID> clear
pcloudc change\-link\-password <LINK-ID>             # prompts securely
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Numeric
.I LINK-ID .
Password is read via the secret\-prompt subsystem when omitted, so it
never appears on argv or shell history.
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with
.B message
=
.BR "public link password updated: id=<N>"
or
.BR "public link password cleared: id=<N>" .
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc change\-link\-password 42 clear
pcloudc change\-link\-password 42 's3cr3t!'
pcloudc change\-link\-password 42
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest ", " 5 Auth ", " 7 Conflict .
.TP
.B Related
.BR change-link-expire ", " change-link-upload ", " show-link .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1.8 create-upload-link \fIREMOTE-FOLDER\fR \fR[\fICOMMENT\fR [\fIEXPIRE\fR [\fIMAXSPACE\fR [\fIMAXFILES\fR]]]]
.TP
.B Purpose
Reverse\-direction link: third parties
.I upload
into a folder you own, without a pCloud account.
.TP
.B Why
Receive submissions (legal exhibits, media drops, client deliverables)
with per\-link quota caps.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc create\-upload\-link <REMOTE-FOLDER>
pcloudc create\-upload\-link <REMOTE-FOLDER> <COMMENT>
pcloudc create\-upload\-link <REMOTE-FOLDER> <COMMENT> <EXPIRE-UNIX|none>
pcloudc create\-upload\-link <REMOTE-FOLDER> <COMMENT> <EXPIRE> <MAXSPACE|none>
pcloudc create\-upload\-link <REMOTE-FOLDER> <COMMENT> <EXPIRE> <MAXSPACE> <MAXFILES|none>
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Positional caps all accept
.B none
for "no limit"; MAXSPACE is in bytes, MAXFILES is a count.
.TP
.B Output shape
Same JSON as
.BR create-file-link ,
with
.B is_folder
forced to
.BR true :
.RS
.nf
{"id":<u64>,"code":"<str>","is_folder":true,"link":"<url>"}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc create\-upload\-link /Intake
pcloudc create\-upload\-link /Intake "vendor Q1 submissions"
pcloudc create\-upload\-link /Intake "30d drop" 1798675200 10737418240 500
pcloudc create\-upload\-link /Intake "" none 536870912 none
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(empty path; non\-numeric cap),
.B 5 Auth ", " 4 Network .
.TP
.B Related
.BR list-upload-links ", " delete-upload-link ", " change-link-upload .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1.9 list-upload-links
.TP
.B Purpose
Enumerate only the upload\-only links (subset of
.BR list-links ).
.TP
.B Why
Monitor inbox\-style drop zones separately from outbound share links.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc list\-upload\-links
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape
Unlike
.BR list-links ,
.B list-upload-links
returns a free\-text
.B message
(the runtime has not yet been converted to the JSON\-in\-message format):
.RS
.nf
upload links: count=<N>, {id=<u64>, code="...", name="...",
  comment="...", files=<N>, maxspace=<Some(bytes)|None>,
  link="<url>"}, ...
.fi
.RE
Scripts should prefer
.B list-links
and filter
.BR .is_upload ==true .
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc list\-upload\-links
pcloudc \-\-json list\-links | jq '.message | fromjson | .links[] | select(.is_upload)'
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 5 Auth ", " 4 Network .
.TP
.B Related
.BR list-links ", " create-upload-link ", " delete-upload-link .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1.10 delete-upload-link \fILINK-ID\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Revoke an upload\-link.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc delete\-upload\-link <LINK-ID>
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
.B delete-upload-link
requires the
.I numeric
link id (unlike
.BR delete-link ,
it does NOT accept the short code).
.BR list-upload-links
surfaces the id via its
.B id=
token.
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with
.B message
=
.BR "upload link deleted: id=<N>" .
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc delete\-upload\-link 42
# Revoke all upload-links listed today:
pcloudc \-\-json list\-upload\-links \\
  | grep \-oE 'id=[0-9]+, code=' | cut \-d= \-f2 | cut \-d, \-f1 \\
  | xargs \-r \-n1 pcloudc delete\-upload\-link
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(non\-numeric id),
.B 5 Auth ", " 7 Conflict .
.TP
.B Related
.BR list-upload-links ", " delete-link .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 1.11 publink-send \fICODE\fR \-\-to \fIEMAIL\fR \-\-message \fIMSG\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Mail an existing public link from the daemon to one or more recipients
(alias:
.BR send-publink ).
.TP
.B Why
The mail shows up from your pCloud identity and inherits pCloud's
anti\-abuse controls, unlike client\-side mail.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc publink\-send <CODE> \-\-to <EMAIL>[,<EMAIL>...] \-\-message <MSG>
pcloudc send\-publink <CODE> \-\-to <EMAIL>[,<EMAIL>...] \-\-message <MSG>
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
.I CODE
must belong to the current account. Mirrors C
.BR psync_send_publink .
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a free\-text confirmation naming the code and recipient count.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc publink\-send XKzA1B2C3 \-\-to alice@example.com \-\-message "See attached"
pcloudc publink\-send XKzA1B2C3 \-\-to "a@x.com,b@x.com" \-\-message "Q1 reports"
pcloudc \-\-json publink\-send XKzA1B2C3 \-\-to ops@example.com \-\-message "restore"
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(empty code, malformed email),
.B 5 Auth ", " 7 Conflict ,
.B 4 Network .
.TP
.B Related
.BR show-link ", " create-file-link ", " list-links .
.\" =====================================================================
.SS 2. Shares (concept primer)
.PP
Shares are
.B account\-to\-account
folder grants: both sides must have a pCloud identity. Typical shape:
owner sends a
.B share request
to the recipient's email; recipient
.B accept-share-request
it and the folder appears under
.IR Shared/ .
Permissions are a bitmask
.RB ( 1=read, 2=create, 4=modify, 8=delete, 16=manage ).
Sharing into a crypto folder requires the temp\-pass handshake (Rust
.IR pcloud-crypto/share_temppass.rs ).
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 2.1 share-folder \fIFOLDER-ID\fR \fIEMAIL\fR \fIPERMS\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Invite a contact to a remote folder with a chosen permission set.
.TP
.B Why
Grant ongoing collaborative access, vs. a one\-off public link.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc share\-folder <FOLDER-ID> <EMAIL> <PERMS>
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
.I FOLDER-ID
numeric (resolve paths via
.BR "folder id " \fIPATH\fR );
.I PERMS
is a positive integer bitmask.
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with confirmation text including folder id and recipient email.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
FID=$(pcloudc folder id /Projects/Atlas)
pcloudc share\-folder "$FID" alice@example.com 1       # read-only
pcloudc share\-folder "$FID" alice@example.com 7       # read+create+modify
pcloudc share\-folder "$FID" alice@example.com 15      # +delete
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest ", " 5 Auth ", " 7 Conflict
(duplicate invite; locked crypto folder without temp\-pass).
.TP
.B Related
.BR list-outgoing-share-requests ", " cancel-share-request ", " modify-share ", " remove-share .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 2.2 list-incoming-shares, list-outgoing-shares
.TP
.B Purpose
Enumerate active (already accepted) shares. Incoming = folders others
granted you; outgoing = folders you granted others.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc list\-incoming\-shares
pcloudc list\-outgoing\-shares
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape
Free\-text
.B message
rows with
.BR share_id ,
.BR folderid ,
.BR email ,
and permission bits.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc list\-incoming\-shares
pcloudc list\-outgoing\-shares
pcloudc \-\-json list\-outgoing\-shares | jq '.message' \-r
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 5 Auth ", " 4 Network .
.TP
.B Related
.BR share-folder ", " modify-share ", " remove-share .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 2.3 list-incoming-share-requests, list-outgoing-share-requests
.TP
.B Purpose
Show pending (not yet accepted/declined) share invitations.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc list\-incoming\-share\-requests
pcloudc list\-outgoing\-share\-requests
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape
Free\-text rows with
.BR sharerequestid ,
.BR email ,
.BR folderid ,
.BR permissions .
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc list\-incoming\-share\-requests
pcloudc list\-outgoing\-share\-requests
pcloudc \-\-json list\-incoming\-share\-requests
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 5 Auth ", " 4 Network .
.TP
.B Related
.BR accept-share-request ", " decline-share-request ", " cancel-share-request .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 2.4 accept-share-request \fISHARE-REQ-ID\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Accept a pending incoming share; the folder appears under
.I /Shared/
in the recipient's tree.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc accept\-share\-request <SHARE-REQ-ID>
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
.I SHARE-REQ-ID
from
.BR list-incoming-share-requests .
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
confirming acceptance with the resulting
.BR share_id .
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc accept\-share\-request 101
pcloudc \-\-json accept\-share\-request 101
pcloudc list\-incoming\-share\-requests | awk '/sharerequestid=/{print $2}'
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest ", " 5 Auth ", " 7 Conflict
(already accepted/declined).
.TP
.B Related
.BR decline-share-request ", " list-incoming-shares .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 2.5 decline-share-request \fISHARE-REQ-ID\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Refuse an incoming share.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc decline\-share\-request <SHARE-REQ-ID>
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a confirmation message.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc decline\-share\-request 101
pcloudc \-\-json decline\-share\-request 101
# Bulk-decline all pending inbound invites:
pcloudc list\-incoming\-share\-requests \\
  | awk '/sharerequestid=/{print $2}' | tr \-d , \\
  | xargs \-r \-n1 pcloudc decline\-share\-request
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest ", " 5 Auth ", " 7 Conflict .
.TP
.B Related
.BR accept-share-request ", " cancel-share-request .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 2.6 cancel-share-request \fISHARE-REQ-ID\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Retract a share invitation you sent, before the recipient accepts it.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc cancel\-share\-request <SHARE-REQ-ID>
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a confirmation line.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc cancel\-share\-request 102
pcloudc \-\-json cancel\-share\-request 102
pcloudc list\-outgoing\-share\-requests
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest ", " 5 Auth ", " 7 Conflict
(request already accepted or declined).
.TP
.B Related
.BR list-outgoing-share-requests ", " share-folder .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 2.7 modify-share \fISHARE-ID\fR \fIPERMS\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Change an existing collaborator's permission bitmask.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc modify\-share <SHARE-ID> <PERMS>
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
You must be the share owner.
.I SHARE-ID
from
.BR list-outgoing-shares .
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with the resulting bitmask.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc modify\-share 77 1       # downgrade to read-only
pcloudc modify\-share 77 15      # promote to +delete
pcloudc modify\-share 77 7       # read+create+modify
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest ", " 5 Auth ", " 7 Conflict .
.TP
.B Related
.BR remove-share ", " list-outgoing-shares .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 2.8 remove-share \fISHARE-ID\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Revoke an accepted share entirely.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc remove\-share <SHARE-ID>
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a confirmation line.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc remove\-share 77
pcloudc \-\-json remove\-share 77
# Owner-side cleanup of every outgoing share:
pcloudc \-\-json list\-outgoing\-shares \\
  | jq \-r '.message' | grep \-oE 'share_id=[0-9]+' | cut \-d= \-f2 \\
  | xargs \-r \-n1 pcloudc remove\-share
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest ", " 5 Auth ", " 7 Conflict .
.TP
.B Related
.BR modify-share ", " cancel-share-request .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 2.9 account-teamshare, account-stopshare, account-modifyshare
.TP
.B Purpose
Business / team\-account variants of share operations; grantee is a
.B team
rather than a single user.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc account\-teamshare <FOLDER-ID> <TEAM-ID> <PERMS>
pcloudc account\-stopshare <SHARE-ID>
pcloudc account\-modifyshare <SHARE-ID> <PERMS>
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Business\-plan account; caller has
.B manage
on the folder;
.I TEAM-ID
via
.BR list-myteams .
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a confirmation line. Crypto\-aware variants preserve the temp\-pass
handshake for encrypted folders.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
TID=$(pcloudc list\-myteams | awk '/teamid=17/{print $1}')
pcloudc account\-teamshare 12345 "$TID" 7
pcloudc account\-modifyshare 77 15
pcloudc account\-stopshare 77
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 5 Auth ,
.B 7 Conflict
(not a business account; missing
.B manage
bit; crypto folder without temp\-pass).
.TP
.B Related
.BR list-myteams ", " share-folder ", " modify-share .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 2.10 list-contacts, list-myteams
.TP
.B Purpose
.BR list-contacts
shows previously\-shared\-with addresses (address\-book);
.BR list-myteams
shows teams the current account belongs to (business plans).
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc list\-contacts
pcloudc list\-myteams
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a free\-text
.B message .
.BR list-contacts
rows surface
.BR email
and usage counters;
.BR list-myteams
rows surface
.BR teamid
and
.BR teamname .
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc list\-contacts
pcloudc list\-myteams
pcloudc \-\-json list\-myteams | jq '.message' \-r
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 5 Auth ", " 4 Network ,
or (for
.BR list-myteams
on non\-business accounts)
.BR 7 Conflict .
.TP
.B Related
.BR share-folder ", " account-teamshare .
.\" =====================================================================
.SS 3. Backup snapshots (see \fBSNAPSHOTS\fR section)
.PP
The
.B snapshot
family (tar \(-> zstd \(-> SHA3\-256 \(-> sidecar manifest, optional GPG wrap)
has its own dedicated section later in this page \(em see
.B SNAPSHOTS
below for the concept primer, the full
.BR "snapshot create" ", "
.BR "snapshot verify" ", "
.BR "snapshot restore" ", and "
.BR "snapshot prune"
templates, compression\-level guidance, GPG interaction, and the
deprecation note for the legacy
.BR "backup snapshot-*"
aliases. The legacy surface is still accepted but prints a stderr
deprecation warning on every invocation.
.PP
.B "backup delete " \fIBACKUP_ID\fR
.TP
Delete a server-side backup record by numeric ID. Purges the daemon's local
state for that backup device and issues a
.B DeleteBackup
IPC request to the daemon, which calls the pCloud
.B deletebackup
API. Mirrors C
.BR psync_delete_backup .
.RS
.TP
.I BACKUP_ID
Unsigned integer backup-device ID returned by the
.B backup list
command or by the pCloud web console.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc backup list                    # find the ID
pcloudc backup delete 42
pcloudc \-\-json backup delete 42 | jq .status
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 Usage
(non-numeric or missing ID),
.B 5 Auth
(session expired),
.B 7 Conflict
(ID not found on server).
.RE
.PP
There is currently
.B no
.B "backup create"
CLI subcommand. Initial backup-device registration runs at
.B login
or is exercised via the SDK
.RB ( psync_register_backup_device / psync_create_backup ).
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.\" =====================================================================
.SS 4. Crypto folder (concept primer)
.PP
Client\-side
.B AES-256-GCM
encryption of a dedicated "crypto folder" tree under your account.
Plaintext file and folder names are also encrypted (metadata filenames
are deterministic ciphertext). Keys are derived from your
.B crypto password ;
the password never leaves the daemon, and
.BR SecretString / SecretBytes
wrappers zeroize key material on drop. Lifecycle:
.BR Uninitialised -> Initialised -> Started -> Unlocked .
.PP
.B Available CLI surface (verified against
.IR crates/pcloud-cli/src/app.rs "):"
.RS
.TP
.B crypto start
unlock the crypto root. Alias: root\-level
.BR unlock-crypto .
.TP
.B crypto stop
re\-lock the crypto root. Alias: root\-level
.BR lock-crypto .
.TP
.B crypto status
print setup/started/unlock state, folder count, hint, safe\-policy flag.
.RE
.PP
.B Full CLI surface
(verified against
.IR crates/pcloud-cli/src/app.rs ):
.BR "crypto start" ", " "crypto stop" ", " "crypto status" ", " "crypto reset" ", " "crypto hint" ", " "crypto priv-key-flags" ", " "crypto send-change-private" ", " "crypto change-password" ", " "crypto change-password-unlocked" .
Initial crypto setup runs during
.B login
with
.BR \-\-crypto .
Root-level aliases
.BR unlock-crypto / lock-crypto
are still accepted alongside
.BR "crypto start" / "crypto stop" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 4.1 crypto start (alias: unlock-crypto)
.TP
.B Purpose
Unlock the crypto folder for the current daemon session; password is
read via secret\-prompt (never visible on argv).
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc crypto start
pcloudc unlock\-crypto                # canonical alias
pcloudc unlock\-crypto <PW>           # non-interactive (avoid in shells)
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Crypto root already set up (see login
.BR \-\-crypto ).
Without that, the daemon returns
.BR "7 Conflict " "with a setup hint."
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with
.BR "crypto unlocked: folders=<N>" ;
.B 5 Auth
with the password\-retry counter on a wrong password.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc crypto start
pcloudc crypto status
pcloudc \-\-json unlock\-crypto
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 5 Auth
(wrong password),
.B 7 Conflict
(crypto not set up),
.B 8 Internal
(key vault I/O).
.TP
.B Related
.BR "crypto stop" ", " "crypto status" ", " login .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 4.2 crypto stop (alias: lock-crypto)
.TP
.B Purpose
Re\-lock the crypto folder immediately; in\-memory keys are zeroized.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc crypto stop
pcloudc lock\-crypto
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
None beyond an authenticated session; locking a locked folder is a
no\-op with
.B status=Ok .
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a confirmation line.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc crypto stop
pcloudc lock\-crypto
systemctl \-\-user start pcloud-lock.service   # wraps `pcloudc lock-crypto`
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 4 Network
(daemon unreachable),
.B 8 Internal
(zeroize path error; very rare).
.TP
.B Related
.BR "crypto start" ", " "crypto status" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 4.3 crypto status
.TP
.B Purpose
Report the current crypto subsystem state without mutating it.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc crypto status
pcloudc crypto st                     # abbreviated
pcloudc crypto\-status                 # canonical hyphenated form
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with free\-text
.B message
(verified against
.BR runtime.rs:1769 ):
.RS
.nf
crypto: setup=<bool>, started=<bool>, state=<Locked|Unlocked|...>,
  folders=<N>, hint=<string|"<none>">, policy_safe=<bool>
.fi
.RE
Parse the
.BR state=
and
.BR folders=
tokens; under
.B \-\-json
the envelope preserves the whole line verbatim in
.BR .message .
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc crypto status
pcloudc \-\-field 'message' crypto status
pcloudc crypto status | grep \-q 'state=Unlocked' && echo ok
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
Effectively none in steady state;
.B 4 Network
if the daemon is down.
.TP
.B Related
.BR "crypto start" ", " "crypto stop" ", " login .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 4.4 crypto reset
.TP
.B Purpose
Wipe the local crypto fingerprint cache and folder registry.
This is a
.B recovery-only
operation \(em the server-side AES-256-GCM ciphertext is
.B not
deleted. After a reset the next
.B crypto start
re-downloads and re-verifies the fingerprint from the server.
Mirrors C
.BR psync_crypto_reset .
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc crypto reset
pcloudc crypto-reset                  # canonical hyphenated alias
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Authenticated session. Crypto may be in any state (locked, unlocked, or
uninitialised). The daemon stops the crypto subsystem first if currently
running.
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a confirmation string.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc crypto reset
pcloudc crypto status                 # confirm state is Uninitialised
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 8 Internal
(store I/O),
.B 4 Network
(daemon unreachable).
.TP
.B Related
.BR "crypto start" ", " "crypto status" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 4.5 crypto hint
.TP
.B Purpose
Fetch the free-text passphrase hint stored during initial crypto setup.
The hint is
.B never
the passphrase itself; it is a user-chosen mnemonic. Requires crypto to be
set up, but
.B not
unlocked.
Mirrors C
.BR psync_crypto_get_hint .
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc crypto hint
pcloudc crypto-hint
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Crypto set up (hint exists). If crypto was never set up the daemon returns
.B 7 Conflict .
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with the hint string in
.BR .message .
Empty message if no hint was set at setup time.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc crypto hint
pcloudc \-\-json crypto hint | jq \-r .message
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 7 Conflict
(crypto not set up),
.B 4 Network
(daemon unreachable).
.TP
.B Related
.BR "crypto status" ", " "crypto change-password" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 4.6 crypto priv-key-flags
.TP
.B Purpose
Return the current
.B crypto_private_flags
integer. The bitmask encodes metadata about the private-key state; the
most useful bit is
.B PSYNC_CRYPTO_FLAG_TEMP_PASS
which is set while a temporary password is in effect (e.g. after account
recovery). Mirrors C
.BR psync_crypto_priv_key_flags .
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc crypto priv-key-flags
pcloudc crypto-priv-key-flags
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Authenticated session; crypto must be set up.
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with the decimal integer in
.BR .message .
Bit 0 = TEMP_PASS.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc crypto priv-key-flags
pcloudc \-\-json crypto priv-key-flags | jq .message
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 7 Conflict
(crypto not set up),
.B 4 Network
(daemon unreachable).
.TP
.B Related
.BR "crypto hint" ", " "crypto change-password" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 4.7 crypto send-change-private
.TP
.B Purpose
Request a server-side one-time confirmation code authorising a subsequent
.B crypto change-password
call. The code is delivered out-of-band (e.g. by email or SMS). Run this
.B first ,
then run
.B crypto change-password
once you have received the code. Mirrors C
.BR psync_crypto_send_change_user_private .
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc crypto send-change-private
pcloudc crypto-send-change-private
pcloudc crypto send-change               # short alias
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Authenticated session; crypto set up. The daemon throttles repeated
calls server-side.
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a confirmation string.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc crypto send-change-private
# wait for email, then:
pcloudc crypto change-password
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 5 Auth
(session expired),
.B 8 Internal
(server refused / throttle).
.TP
.B Related
.BR "crypto change-password" ", " "crypto priv-key-flags" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 4.8 crypto change-password \fR[\fIOLD\fR [\fINEW\fR [\fIHINT\fR [\fICODE\fR]]]]
.TP
.B Purpose
Rotate the crypto passphrase.
The crypto folder may be locked \(em the old password is verified on the
server side before any change is applied. Prompts interactively for:
old passphrase, new passphrase, hint, and the server confirmation code from
.BR "crypto send-change-private" .
All passphrases are transit-only secrets;
.B none
are written to disk.
Mirrors C
.BR psync_crypto_change_crypto_pass .
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc crypto change-password
pcloudc crypto-change-password
# Interactive prompts for OLD, NEW, HINT, CODE
.fi
.TP
.B Positional arguments
.RS
.TP
.I OLD
Current crypto passphrase. Prompted securely if omitted.
.TP
.I NEW
Replacement crypto passphrase. Prompted securely if omitted.
.TP
.I HINT
Free-text mnemonic stored with the new passphrase.
.TP
.I CODE
Confirmation code from
.BR "crypto send-change-private" .
.RE
.TP
.B Flags
.B \-\-password-stdin
reads the old passphrase from stdin (pipe-friendly);
.B \-\-password-env
reads it from
.BR PCLOUD_PASSWORD .
.TP
.B Preconditions
Crypto set up. Confirmation code obtained via
.BR "crypto send-change-private" .
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a confirmation string on success.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc crypto send-change-private       # request code first
pcloudc crypto change-password           # interactive
echo "$OLD" | pcloudc \-\-password\-stdin crypto change-password
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 5 Auth
(wrong old passphrase),
.B 7 Conflict
(bad code or crypto not set up),
.B 8 Internal
(key-store write failure).
.TP
.B Related
.BR "crypto send-change-private" ", " "crypto hint" ", " "crypto status" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 4.9 crypto change-password-unlocked \fR[\fINEW\fR [\fIHINT\fR [\fICODE\fR]]]
.TP
.B Purpose
Same as
.B crypto change-password
but valid
.B only
when the crypto folder is already unlocked; the old passphrase is not
required because the session key is already in memory.
Mirrors C
.BR psync_crypto_change_crypto_pass_unlocked .
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc crypto change-password-unlocked
pcloudc crypto-change-password-unlocked
.fi
.TP
.B Positional arguments
.RS
.TP
.I NEW
Replacement crypto passphrase. Prompted securely if omitted.
.TP
.I HINT
Free-text mnemonic stored with the new passphrase.
.TP
.I CODE
Confirmation code from
.BR "crypto send-change-private" .
.RE
.TP
.B Preconditions
Crypto
.B unlocked
(crypto start already run in this session).
Confirmation code obtained via
.BR "crypto send-change-private" .
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a confirmation string on success.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc crypto start
pcloudc crypto send-change-private
pcloudc crypto change-password-unlocked  # no old-pass prompt
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 7 Conflict
(crypto not unlocked or bad code),
.B 8 Internal
(key-store write).
.TP
.B Related
.BR "crypto change-password" ", " "crypto start" ", " "crypto send-change-private" .
.\" =====================================================================
.SS 4.10 Account management
.PP
The
.B account
group exposes lifecycle and preference helpers for the pCloud account
attached to the running daemon session. All subcommands are two-token
forms
.RB ( "pcloudc account <sub>" )
or single-token forms
.RB ( "pcloudc account-<sub>" ).
Unless noted otherwise an authenticated session is required; the daemon
must be running.
.TP
.B account verify-email
.RB "(single\-token: " account-verify-email )
Trigger the server to send a verification email to the account address.
Does not require any arguments. Mirrors C
.BR psync_verify_email .
.TP
.B "account verify-email-restricted " \fITOKEN\fR
.RB "(single\-token: " account-verify-restricted )
Submit a restricted verification token (delivered out-of-band by the
pCloud mail flow). Mirrors C
.BR psync_verify_email_restricted .
.TP
.B "account lost-password " \fIEMAIL\fR
.RB "(aliases: " "account reset-password" ", " "account forgot-password" )
Request a password-reset link for
.IR EMAIL .
Does
.B not
require an existing authenticated session \(em use it when the user has
forgotten their password before login. Mirrors C
.BR psync_lost_password .
.TP
.B "account change-password"
.RB "(single\-token: " account-change-password )
Rotate the account (not crypto) password. Prompts for the current password
and then the new password via secure prompt. Mirrors C
.BR psync_change_password .
.RS
.TP
.B Flags
.B \-\-password-stdin ,
.B \-\-password-env
Read the current password non-interactively.
.RE
.TP
.B "account register " \fIEMAIL\fR
.RB "(single\-token: " account-register )
Create a new pCloud account with the given
.I EMAIL .
Prompts for a password. Pass
.B \-\-accept-terms
to confirm you have read the pCloud Terms of Service; the server rejects
registrations without it. Mirrors C
.BR psync_register .
.RS
.TP
.B Flags
.B \-\-accept-terms
Required. Asserts acceptance of the pCloud ToS.
.RE
.TP
.B account api-servers
.RB "(aliases: " account-api-servers ", " "account apiservers" )
List available pCloud API server endpoints (location IDs, hostnames, and
binapi addresses). Useful for configuring multi-region or EU-only access.
Mirrors C
.BR psync_get_api_servers .
.TP
.B "account set-api-server " \fILOCATION_ID\fR \fIBINAPI\fR
.RB "(aliases: " account-set-api-server ", " "account set-server" )
Pin the daemon's API endpoint to a specific server.
.I LOCATION_ID
is an unsigned integer (from
.BR "account api-servers" );
.I BINAPI
is the binapi hostname string (e.g.
.IR "binapi.pcloud.com" ).
Mirrors C
.BR psync_set_api_server .
.TP
.B "account set-language " \fILANG\fR
.RB "(aliases: " account-set-language ", " "account language" )
Set the account UI language to the BCP-47 tag
.I LANG
(e.g.
.IR en ,
.IR de ,
.IR fr ).
Mirrors C
.BR psync_set_language .
.TP
.B account promo
.RB "(single\-token: " account-promo )
Fetch any active promotional offer attached to this account. Returns
.B status=Ok
with the promo details in
.B .message
(or an empty message if no promo is active). Mirrors C
.BR psync_get_promo .
.PP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc account verify-email
pcloudc account lost-password user@example.com
pcloudc account change-password
pcloudc account register user@example.com \-\-accept-terms
pcloudc account api-servers
pcloudc account set-api-server 1 binapi.pcloud.com
pcloudc account set-language de
pcloudc account promo
pcloudc \-\-json account api-servers | jq '.[].hostname'
.fi
.\" =====================================================================
.SS 4.11 Downloads
.PP
The
.B download
group (alias:
.BR dl )
provides helpers for fetching files from your pCloud account to local disk
without going through a sync root.
.TP
.B "download link " \fIFILE_ID\fR
.RB "(single\-token: " download-link ", aliases: " "dl link" )
Obtain a signed, time-limited HTTPS download URL for the file with numeric
.IR FILE_ID .
The URL is printed to stdout \(em it is not fetched. Pipe it to
.BR curl (1)
or your download manager. Mirrors C
.BR psync_get_file_link .
.RS
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc download link 123456
curl \-L "$(pcloudc download link 123456)" \-o file.bin
pcloudc \-\-json download link 123456 | jq \-r .url
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 Usage
(non-numeric or missing FILE_ID),
.B 5 Auth
(session expired),
.B 7 Conflict
(file not found or no read permission).
.RE
.TP
.B "download file " \fIFILE_ID\fR \fILOCAL_PATH\fR
.RB "(single\-token: " download-file ", aliases: " "dl file" )
Fetch the file with numeric
.I FILE_ID
from pCloud and write it to
.IR LOCAL_PATH .
The daemon resolves the signed download URL and streams the bytes; the
local file is created if it does not exist, or overwritten if it does.
Progress is not currently printed to stdout; exit code 0 means the file
was written completely. Mirrors C
.BR psync_download_file .
.RS
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc download file 123456 ~/Downloads/report.pdf
pcloudc dl file 123456 /tmp/output.zip
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 Usage
(missing FILE_ID or LOCAL_PATH),
.B 5 Auth
(session expired),
.B 4 Network
(download interrupted),
.B 8 Internal
(local I/O error — check disk space and path permissions).
.RE
.\" =====================================================================
.SS 5. Integrity sweeper (concept primer)
.PP
Background scrub worker that periodically hashes local files and
compares the result against the server\-side checksum, emitting
.B NDJSON
events for each observation.
.PP
.B Honesty note:
the sweeper is
.B operator\-triggered \(em
there is no daemon\-side auto\-start cron today. Enable it in
.BR pcloud.conf (5)
under
.BR [features.integrity_sweeper]
and drive it from host cron or via
.BR "integrity run-once" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 5.1 integrity status
.TP
.B Purpose
Inspect sweeper state: enabled flag, last\-run timestamp, per\-kind
counters, dropped\-audit count, resolved skip\-list path.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc integrity status
pcloudc integrity st
pcloudc integrity                 # defaults to status
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a free\-text
.B message
naming counters per result kind
.RB ( Ok ", " Mismatch ", " LocalMissing ", " RemoteMissing ", " Throttled ", " FetchFailed ).
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc integrity status
pcloudc \-\-json integrity status
pcloudc \-\-field 'message' integrity status
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 4 Network
(daemon down); otherwise always
.BR 0 Ok .
.TP
.B Related
.BR "integrity run-once" ", " "integrity skip" ", " verify ", " audit-verify .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 5.2 integrity run-once
.TP
.B Purpose
Trigger a single on\-demand sweep and print the delta report.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc integrity run\-once
pcloudc integrity run_once        # underscore alias
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Authenticated session; sweeper enabled in config
.RB ( "[features.integrity_sweeper] enabled = true" ).
Honours the configured rate limit and skip list.
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with per\-kind counters; emits NDJSON events on the daemon audit stream
(one per non\-Ok, non\-Throttled record).
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc integrity run\-once
pcloudc \-\-json integrity run\-once
# Cron:
0 3 * * * pcloudc integrity run\-once >> /var/log/pcloud/integrity.ndjson
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 5 Auth ", " 4 Network ,
.B 6 Unavailable
(sweeper disabled in config).
.TP
.B Related
.BR "integrity status" ", " "integrity skip" ", " verify .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 5.3 integrity skip \fIPATH\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Append
.I PATH
to the on\-disk skip list and reload it; subsequent sweeps will not
touch it.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc integrity skip <PATH>
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
.I PATH
absolute or relative to a sync root. Skip list is persisted on disk;
command is idempotent.
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with the resolved skip\-list path and entry count.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc integrity skip ~/pCloud/build-artifacts
pcloudc integrity skip /home/alice/pCloud/node_modules
pcloudc integrity status
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(empty path),
.B 8 Internal
(skip\-list I/O).
.TP
.B Related
.BR "integrity status" ", " "integrity run-once" .
.\" =====================================================================
.SS 6. verify \fIPATH\fR \fR[\fB\-\-recursive\fR] \fR[\fB\-\-fix\fR] \fR[\fB\-\-yes\fR]
.TP
.B Purpose
One\-shot content verification of a sync root (or single file): hash each
local object and compare to the remote hash, emit one line per object in
a stable taxonomy.
.TP
.B Why
Reconcile after a crash, disk swap, or suspected local tamper; detect
silent corruption.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc verify <PATH>
pcloudc verify <PATH> \-\-recursive
pcloudc verify <PATH> \-\-recursive \-\-fix \-\-yes
pcloudc \-\-json verify <PATH> \-\-recursive
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Path within a known sync root. With
.B \-\-fix
the daemon re\-downloads
.BR MISMATCH / MISSING_LOCAL
entries and re\-uploads
.BR MISSING_REMOTE
entries \(em destructive, so
.B \-\-yes
(or interactive TTY confirmation) is required.
.TP
.B Output shape
One line per record (text mode):
.RS
.nf
[OK]                                 <path>
[MISMATCH local=<hex> server=<hex>]  <path>
[MISSING_LOCAL]                      <path>
[MISSING_REMOTE]                     <path>
.fi
.RE
Under
.B \-\-json
the command streams
.B NDJSON
(one JSON object per line, not a wrapping array).
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc verify ~/Documents
pcloudc verify ~/Documents \-\-recursive
pcloudc \-\-json verify ~/Documents \-\-recursive | jq 'select(.kind == "MISMATCH")'
pcloudc verify ~/Photos \-\-recursive \-\-fix \-\-yes
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 0 Ok
when every record is
.BR [OK] ;
.B 7 Conflict
as soon as any
.B [MISMATCH]
is observed (even with
.BR \-\-fix ,
so cron jobs notice);
.B 6 Unavailable
when the walk completes but one or more records were
.BR MISSING_LOCAL / MISSING_REMOTE
and
.B \-\-fix
was not passed;
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(missing path; path outside a sync root).
.TP
.B Related
.BR "integrity run-once" ", " "audit verify" ", " log ", " restore .
.\" =====================================================================
.SS 7. File history (log / diff / restore) \(em HONESTY NOTE
.PP
.BR log ,
.BR diff ,
and
.BR restore
are
.B scaffolded:
CLI parsing and the daemon's
.B FileHistory
dispatch path are complete, but the backend currently returns
.B "6 Unavailable"
with the message
.BR "file-history: not yet supported by pCloud public API (tracked under bd-1du.10)" .
The underlying pCloud
.B listrevisions
endpoint is undocumented and awaiting public approval; enabling it is a
one\-change\-site operation on
.BR FolderRuntime::list_revisions .
Until then, every call exits
.B 6 Unavailable .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 7.1 log \fIPATH\fR \fR[\fB\-\-limit \fIN\fR]
.TP
.B Purpose
(When implemented) list prior revisions of a single remote file.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc log <PATH>
pcloudc log <PATH> \-\-limit <N>
pcloudc file\-log <PATH>                # alias
pcloudc file\-history <PATH>            # alias
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Authenticated session; path must be a non\-empty absolute remote path
.RB ( "3 InvalidRequest"
on empty).
.TP
.B Output shape (today)
.B status=Unavailable
with the message documented above; exit code
.BR 6 .
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc log /Documents/Plan.md
pcloudc log /Documents/Plan.md \-\-limit 20
# Treat Unavailable as a known TBD, not a failure:
pcloudc log /Documents/Plan.md; [ $? -eq 6 ] && echo "(revision API pending)"
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
Today: always
.BR 6 Unavailable ;
future:
.B 5 Auth ", " 4 Network ", " 3 InvalidRequest .
.TP
.B Related
.BR diff ", " restore .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 7.2 diff \fIPATH\fR \fIREV_A\fR \fIREV_B\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
(When implemented) compare two revision ids (from
.BR log )
for the same remote file.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc diff <PATH> <REV_A> <REV_B>
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape (today)
.B 6 Unavailable .
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc diff /Documents/Plan.md 101 104
pcloudc diff /Reports/Q1.pdf 12 17
pcloudc \-\-json diff /Reports/Q1.pdf 12 17
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
Today:
.B 6 Unavailable .
.TP
.B Related
.BR log ", " restore .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS 7.3 restore \fIPATH\fR \fIREV\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
(When implemented) replace the current remote object with a named prior
revision.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc restore <PATH> <REV>
.fi
.TP
.B Output shape (today)
.B 6 Unavailable .
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc restore /Documents/Plan.md 101
pcloudc restore /Reports/Q1.pdf 12
pcloudc \-\-json restore /Reports/Q1.pdf 12
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
Today:
.B 6 Unavailable .
.TP
.B Related
.BR log ", " diff ", " verify .
.\" =====================================================================
.SS 8. audit verify
.TP
.B Purpose
Verify the local audit log chain: every hash\-chained audit row is
recomputed and the resulting tip is compared to the recorded anchor.
.TP
.B Why
Detect after\-the\-fact tampering of the on\-disk audit log; prove to
auditors that the chain is unbroken.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc audit verify
pcloudc audit\-verify                              # root-level alias
pcloudc audit verify \-\-from <TS> \-\-to <TS>   # future: bounded verify
.fi
.TP
.B Preconditions
Audit log must exist on disk under the configured state\-dir. The
.BR --from / --to
flags are parsed today but bounded verification lands with the final
parity proof (bd\-1du.10); an unbounded call always works.
.TP
.B Output shape
.B status=Ok
with a verification summary (row count, start/end ts, computed tip,
recorded anchor). A broken chain yields
.B 7 Conflict
naming the first bad row.
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc audit verify
pcloudc \-\-json audit verify
# Nightly integrity-of-integrity cron:
0 2 * * * pcloudc audit verify || systemd\-cat \-t pcloud \-p crit \\
          "audit chain broken"
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 7 Conflict
(chain broken),
.B 8 Internal
(audit log unreadable),
.B 4 Network
(daemon unreachable).
.TP
.B Related
.BR verify ", " "integrity status" ", " "snapshot verify" .
.\" =====================================================================
.\" =====================================================================
.SH SNAPSHOTS
.PP
The
.B snapshot
family produces self\-describing, offline\-verifiable point\-in\-time
archives of
.I daemon state
(auth vault, SQLite store, audit chain, config with secret refs redacted,
plugin registry). A snapshot is
.I not
a live data replica \(em it is a cold recovery artifact. Use
.BR sync (see
.BR "sync add" )
to keep files continuously mirrored; use
.BR snapshot
to capture a moment you can restore from later.
.PP
.B Concept primer.
A snapshot is one immutable archive plus one machine\-readable sidecar
manifest. The archive is a tar of the daemon state dir, compressed with
.BR zstd (1)
(level 1\(en22, default 3), then hashed end\-to\-end with
.B SHA3\-256
over the compressed bytes. The hash, the chosen zstd level, the
uncompressed/compressed size pair, the encryption flag, and the
label are written to a sidecar file named
.BR <archive>.manifest.json
living next to the archive. If
.B \-\-gpg\-recipient
is supplied the archive is additionally GPG\-wrapped and emitted as
.B <archive>.gpg
(extension
.BR .tar.zst.gpg );
the SHA3 in the manifest remains the digest of the
.I ciphertext
bytes so verification is a single hash comparison even on encrypted
artifacts.
.PP
.B How this differs from \fRsync\fB and from the legacy \fRbackup snapshot-*\fB form.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B sync
is online, continuous, and mutates both sides (local \(<->
remote). A sync root is not a recovery artifact; it is a live mirror.
Losing the daemon state dir would lose its sync bookkeeping.
.IP \(bu 2
.B backup snapshot-*
(legacy) emitted
.B .tar.gpg
and required
.BR gpg (1)
unconditionally. It is still accepted as an alias for the new
.B snapshot
verbs and prints a stderr deprecation warning on every call; see
.B Deprecation
below.
.IP \(bu 2
.B snapshot
(new) makes GPG optional, makes SHA3\-256 mandatory, and writes an
offline\-parseable manifest. You can verify, restore, and prune without
.BR gpg (1)
installed as long as the archive itself is unencrypted.
.RE
.PP
.B Pipeline.
.RS
.nf
state-dir/  \-\->  tar  \-\->  zstd(level N)  \-\->  SHA3-256(compressed bytes)  \-\->  sidecar manifest
                                                        |
                                                        +\-\->  [optional]  GPG(recipient)  \-\->  <archive>.gpg
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B HONESTY NOTE.
The CLI surface
.RB ( "snapshot create" ", " "snapshot verify" ", " "snapshot restore" ", " "snapshot prune" )
parses and validates arguments and emits the stable envelope today, but
the daemon\-side tar/zstd/SHA3 executor is the same pre\-alpha pipeline
tracked under
.BR bd-1du.10 ;
on some builds
.B Request::Snapshot
may still return
.B 6 Unavailable
while the wire format is finalised. CLI \(-> IPC \(-> daemon contract
is stable; cron wrappers written against this section will keep working
once the executor lands.
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS snapshot \(em bare form
.PP
.B snapshot
with no subcommand is accepted as a shortcut for
.B "snapshot create"
against the default artifact path from
.BR pcloud.conf (5)
.RB ( backup.artifact_dir ).
It is
.I not
a dry\-run and
.I not
a status query. Prefer the explicit form in scripts:
.BR "snapshot create <PATH>" .
.RS
.nf
pcloudc snapshot                         # == snapshot create <default>
pcloudc snapshot create /tmp/now.tar.zst # explicit, recommended
.fi
.RE
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS snapshot create \fIPATH\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Produce a new zstd\-compressed, SHA3\-256\-stamped state snapshot at
.IR PATH ,
with a sidecar manifest at
.IR PATH.manifest.json
(or
.IR PATH.gpg.manifest.json
when
.B \-\-gpg\-recipient
is used).
.TP
.B Why
Run before a risky upgrade, on a nightly/weekly cron, or on demand as a
recovery baseline. Compared to the legacy
.B backup snapshot-create
form it is GPG\-optional, smaller on disk at higher
.B \-\-zstd\-level ,
and the manifest lets an operator verify the archive without touching
the daemon.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc snapshot create <PATH> [\-\-zstd\-level <1..22>] \\
                               [\-\-gpg\-recipient <EMAIL>] \\
                               [\-\-label <STRING>]
.fi
.TP
.B Flags
.BR "\-\-zstd\-level <N>"
(1\(en22; default 3; see
.B Choosing a compression level
below),
.BR "\-\-gpg\-recipient <EMAIL>"
(GPG\-wrap the archive; omit to leave the artifact as plain
.BR .tar.zst ),
.BR "\-\-label <STRING>"
(free\-form label persisted into the manifest;
.I not
a secret \(em treated as user\-visible text).
.TP
.B Preconditions
.I PATH
parent directory exists and is writable.
.BR zstd (1)
is bundled (statically linked) in the daemon \(em no host zstd needed.
If
.B \-\-gpg\-recipient
is used then
.BR gpg (1)
MUST be on
.B $PATH
and the recipient key MUST be in the invoking user's keyring;
.B fail\-closed:
a missing
.BR gpg (1)
returns
.B 8 Internal
rather than silently writing an unencrypted archive.
.TP
.B Output shape (JSON, via \-\-json)
.nf
{
  "command": "snapshot-create",
  "status": "ok",
  "exit_code": 0,
  "message": {
    "artifact_path":  "/var/backups/pcloud-rs/2026-04-15.tar.zst",
    "manifest_path":  "/var/backups/pcloud-rs/2026-04-15.tar.zst.manifest.json",
    "sha3_256":       "c9f1\&...b2e0",
    "zstd_level":     3,
    "encrypted":      false,
    "size_bytes":     41287654,
    "uncompressed_bytes": 112903168,
    "label":          "pre-upgrade-2026-04-15"
  }
}
.fi
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
# Default balanced level.
pcloudc snapshot create /var/backups/pcloud-rs/now.tar.zst

# Archival level, labelled.
pcloudc snapshot create /var/backups/pcloud-rs/nightly.tar.zst \\
  \-\-zstd\-level 19 \\
  \-\-label "pre-upgrade-$(date \-u +%F)"

# Encrypted to an offsite key.
pcloudc snapshot create /var/backups/pcloud-rs/dr.tar.zst \\
  \-\-gpg\-recipient dr-team@example.com

# Scripted: just grab the hash and size (see FIELD SELECTION).
pcloudc \-\-field sha3_256 \-\-field size_bytes \\
  snapshot create /tmp/a.tar.zst
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(missing/unwritable
.IR PATH ,
.B \-\-zstd\-level
out of 1\(en22, bad recipient format),
.B 7 Conflict
(recipient missing from keyring,
.I PATH
already exists and is non\-empty),
.B 8 Internal
.RB ( gpg (1)
missing while
.B \-\-gpg\-recipient
was supplied; disk full mid\-write; SHA3 verify of just\-written archive
fails),
.BR "6 Unavailable " "(pre\-alpha, executor gated)."
.TP
.B Related
.BR "snapshot verify" ", " "snapshot restore" ", " "snapshot prune" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS snapshot verify \fIPATH\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Re\-hash the archive at
.I PATH
and compare against the sidecar manifest; when the archive is GPG\-wrapped
also confirm the GPG signature. Offline \(em never touches the live
state dir, never requires a running daemon.
.TP
.B Why
Run before every
.B snapshot restore ,
and wire into CI so every nightly artifact is proven restorable. A
manifest mismatch means the archive is corrupt or has been tampered
with; do not attempt to restore.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc snapshot verify <PATH> [\-\-manifest <MANIFEST-PATH>]
.fi
.TP
.B Flags
.BR "\-\-manifest <PATH>"
(override sidecar lookup; defaults to
.IR <PATH>.manifest.json ).
.TP
.B Preconditions
.I PATH
is a regular file readable by the caller. If the archive ends in
.B .gpg
then
.BR gpg (1)
MUST be on
.B $PATH
and the
.I private
key MUST be in the keyring. Otherwise no external tools are needed.
.TP
.B Output shape (JSON, via \-\-json)
.nf
{
  "command": "snapshot-verify",
  "status": "ok",
  "exit_code": 0,
  "message": {
    "ok":             true,
    "artifact_path":  "/var/backups/pcloud-rs/2026-04-15.tar.zst",
    "sha3_256":       "c9f1\&...b2e0",
    "zstd_level":     3,
    "encrypted":      false,
    "size_bytes":     41287654,
    "label":          "pre-upgrade-2026-04-15"
  }
}
.fi
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc snapshot verify /var/backups/pcloud-rs/2026-04-15.tar.zst
pcloudc snapshot verify /tmp/dr.tar.zst.gpg
# Bare\-field form (snapshot\-verify is on the bare whitelist).
pcloudc snapshot verify /tmp/a.tar.zst sha3_256 zstd_level encrypted
# Non\-sidecar manifest location.
pcloudc snapshot verify /tmp/a.tar.zst \\
  \-\-manifest /secure/a.tar.zst.manifest.json
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(missing/non\-file
.IR PATH ,
manifest path missing or unparseable),
.B 7 Conflict
(SHA3 mismatch \(em archive corrupt or tampered; GPG signature bad; zstd
magic wrong),
.B 8 Internal
(I/O error reading archive mid\-hash;
.BR gpg (1)
crashed),
.BR "6 Unavailable " "(pre\-alpha)."
.TP
.B Related
.BR "snapshot restore" ", " "snapshot create" ", " "audit verify" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS snapshot restore \fIPATH\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Unpack a
.I verified
snapshot archive over the daemon state directory, after first renaming
the current state dir to a timestamped side\-path so the operation is
reversible within the same filesystem.
.TP
.B Why
Recover daemon state after data loss, or rehydrate a new host from a
prior snapshot. Reads the sidecar manifest, verifies SHA3\-256, (if
encrypted) decrypts via
.BR gpg (1),
decompresses via
.BR zstd (1),
stops any running daemon against the target state dir, atomically
renames the existing state dir to
.BR state-dir.pre-restore.<ts> ,
then moves the verified payload into place. Both the renamed old dir
and the new dir are owner\-only.
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc snapshot restore <PATH> \-\-yes [\-\-manifest <MANIFEST-PATH>]
.fi
.TP
.B Flags
.B \-\-yes
(mandatory for non\-interactive callers; restore is destructive, CLI
gate, daemon repeats),
.BR "\-\-manifest <PATH>"
(override sidecar lookup).
.TP
.B Preconditions
Destructive. The target state dir must be on the same filesystem as the
staging dir or the atomic rename is downgraded to a copy\-then\-swap
(still safe, but slower and needs 2x free space).
.B \-\-yes
is required; the CLI refuses without it even when stdin is a TTY, to
match the cron contract.
.TP
.B Output shape (JSON, via \-\-json)
.nf
{
  "command": "snapshot-restore",
  "status": "ok",
  "exit_code": 0,
  "message": {
    "ok":                  true,
    "restored_state_dir":  "/var/lib/pcloud-rs/state",
    "pre_restore_rename":  "/var/lib/pcloud-rs/state.pre-restore.20260415T043012Z",
    "sha3_256":            "c9f1\&...b2e0",
    "encrypted":           false
  }
}
.fi
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc snapshot restore /var/backups/pcloud-rs/2026-04-15.tar.zst \-\-yes
pcloudc snapshot restore /tmp/dr.tar.zst.gpg \-\-yes
# CI\-safe: verify before restore, never chain without the guard.
pcloudc snapshot verify /tmp/snap.tar.zst \\
  && pcloudc snapshot restore /tmp/snap.tar.zst \-\-yes
# Roll back a failed restore (manual).
mv /var/lib/pcloud-rs/state.pre-restore.20260415T043012Z \\
   /var/lib/pcloud-rs/state
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(missing
.BR \-\-yes ;
missing archive/manifest),
.B 7 Conflict
(SHA3 mismatch; daemon refuses while sync is running on the target state
dir; GPG sig bad),
.B 8 Internal
(rename or move fails mid\-restore \(em pre\-restore sibling is left in
place for manual recovery; disk full during decompress),
.BR "6 Unavailable " "(pre\-alpha)."
.TP
.B Related
.BR "snapshot verify" ", " "snapshot create" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS snapshot prune \fIDIR\fR \-\-retention\-days \fIN\fR
.TP
.B Purpose
Apply a retention policy to the snapshot artifact directory
.IR DIR ,
deleting archive + sidecar pairs older than
.I N
days while guaranteeing the
.I most recent successfully verified
snapshot always survives.
.TP
.B Why
Keep the artifact dir from growing unbounded without ever leaving the
operator zero\-snapshot. Idempotent; safe to run from cron after
.B snapshot create
+
.BR "snapshot verify" .
.TP
.B Syntax
.nf
pcloudc snapshot prune <DIR> \-\-retention\-days <N> \-\-yes
.fi
.TP
.B Flags
.B \-\-retention\-days <N>
(required; positive integer; counts full 24\-hour intervals back from
.IR now ),
.B \-\-yes
(required; prune is destructive).
.TP
.B Preconditions
Destructive. Both flags are mandatory \(em the CLI rejects the call
otherwise (no silent default). The pruner walks
.I DIR
for
.BR *.tar.zst ", " *.tar.zst.gpg ,
pairs each with its sidecar manifest, re\-verifies SHA3 before deleting
(to refuse dropping an archive it cannot first confirm is still
consistent), and refuses to delete the last\-good snapshot even if
it is older than the retention window.
.TP
.B Output shape (JSON, via \-\-json)
.nf
{
  "command": "snapshot-prune",
  "status": "ok",
  "exit_code": 0,
  "message": {
    "dir":            "/var/backups/pcloud-rs",
    "retention_days": 14,
    "kept":           [ "\&...2026-04-15.tar.zst", "\&...2026-04-14.tar.zst" ],
    "pruned":         [ "\&...2026-03-20.tar.zst" ],
    "last_good_kept": "/var/backups/pcloud-rs/2026-04-15.tar.zst"
  }
}
.fi
.TP
.B Examples
.nf
pcloudc snapshot prune /var/backups/pcloud-rs \-\-retention\-days 14 \-\-yes
pcloudc snapshot prune /var/backups/pcloud-rs \-\-retention\-days 7  \-\-yes
# Cron (create \-> verify \-> prune):
0 4 * * *  pcloudc snapshot create /var/backups/pcloud-rs/$(date \-u +\\%F).tar.zst \\
             \-\-zstd\-level 19 && \\
           pcloudc snapshot verify \\
             "$(ls \-t /var/backups/pcloud-rs/*.tar.zst | head \-1)" && \\
           pcloudc snapshot prune /var/backups/pcloud-rs \\
             \-\-retention\-days 30 \-\-yes
.fi
.TP
.B Failure modes
.B 3 InvalidRequest
(missing
.B \-\-retention\-days
or
.BR \-\-yes ;
.I N
not a positive integer;
.I DIR
not a directory),
.B 7 Conflict
(policy would drop last\-good snapshot; pruner refuses; SHA3 mismatch on
a candidate archive \(em left in place),
.B 8 Internal
.RI ( DIR
unwritable mid\-prune),
.BR "6 Unavailable " "(pre\-alpha)."
.TP
.B Related
.BR "snapshot create" ", " "snapshot verify" .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS Choosing a compression level
.PP
.B zstd
level controls the speed/size tradeoff on the
.I compression
side only; decompression speed is roughly constant across levels. The
hash and manifest shape do not change with the level \(em a level\-3
archive and a level\-19 archive from the same state dir have different
.B sha3_256
values (because the ciphertext differs) but are equally verifiable.
.TS
box, tab(|);
l l l.
Level|Profile|Use case
_
1|fast|ad\-hoc, interactive, when disk is cheap and wall\-clock matters
3|balanced (default)|unattended daily backups, general purpose
9|better|weekly archives where CPU is available
19|archival|monthly/quarterly cold backups, offsite retention
22|maximum|long\-term archival, accept multi\-minute compress time
.TE
.PP
Rule of thumb: leave it at
.B 3
until you measure. Levels
.B 19\(en22
are only worthwhile when the artifact will live for months and the
size delta (typically a further 10\(en25% over level 3) justifies the
CPU. Levels
.B 20\(en22
use long\-range mode and more memory; budget accordingly on small
hosts.
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS Using GPG
.PP
When
.B \-\-gpg\-recipient <EMAIL>
is passed to
.BR "snapshot create" ,
the pipeline becomes
.I compress\-before\-encrypt
.RB ( tar " \(-> " zstd " \(-> " GPG ),
which is the only correct ordering: encrypted ciphertext is (by design)
incompressible, so compressing after encrypting would just bloat the
archive. Output filename gains the
.B .gpg
suffix, giving the full form
.BR .tar.zst.gpg .
.PP
The
.B SHA3\-256
in the manifest is taken over the
.I ciphertext
bytes (the final
.B .tar.zst.gpg
on disk), not over the plaintext tarball. Consequences:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B snapshot verify
on an encrypted artifact can confirm bit\-for\-bit integrity
.I without
decrypting (the SHA3 compare is enough); decryption is only exercised
when you restore.
.IP \(bu 2
Re\-encrypting the same plaintext with a different key produces a
different
.B sha3_256
because the ciphertext differs. Snapshots are
.I not
content\-addressed across recipients.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR gpg (1)
MUST be on
.B $PATH
for
.B snapshot create
.RB ( \-\-gpg\-recipient )
and for
.B snapshot restore
of an encrypted archive.
.B snapshot verify
of an encrypted archive only needs
.BR gpg (1)
if you actually want to replay the decrypt; the default SHA3\-only
verify has no gpg dependency. Treat encrypted snapshots containing the
auth vault as equivalent to the vault itself.
.RE
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS Deprecation of \fRbackup snapshot-*\fR
.PP
The legacy
.B backup snapshot-create ,
.B backup snapshot-verify ,
.B backup snapshot-restore ,
and
.B backup snapshot-prune
verbs are still accepted and are routed to the new
.B snapshot
pipeline unchanged, but every invocation prints a one\-line stderr
deprecation warning
.RB ( "\(dqbackup snapshot-* is deprecated; use snapshot ...\(dq" )
and an audit event
.B SnapshotLegacyVerbUsed .
The aliases will be removed in a future major release; migrate cron
jobs and runbooks to the
.B snapshot
verbs now. No behavioural change is implied \(em same daemon handler,
same artifact layout, same exit codes.
.\" =====================================================================
.SH FIELD SELECTION
.PP
.B pcloudc
ships a built\-in, dependency\-free field selector so that shell scripts,
cron jobs, CI pipelines, and systemd timers can pick a single value out
of a daemon response without pulling in
.BR jq (1)
or a Python interpreter. It is deliberately narrow: a
.I dotted path
walked against the already\-parsed response
.BR message .
There are no filters, pipes, slices, comma splits, map operations,
assignments, or
.B @csv /
.B @tsv
transforms. If you need any of those, emit
.B \-\-json
and pipe it to whichever richer tool you already run; the envelope shape
is stable within a major version.
.PP
.B Concept primer
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A
.I selector
is a sequence of dot\-separated
.I segments .
Each segment is either an object key (string) or an array index (integer,
0\-based). `\fBlinks.0.code\fR' means "object key \fIlinks\fR, then array
index \fI0\fR, then object key \fIcode\fR".
.IP \(bu 2
Selectors are case\-sensitive; numeric object keys (the rare response
shape `\fB{"0": "zero"}\fR') cannot be addressed because a bare integer
segment is always parsed as an array index. This is an intentional
limitation documented in the code.
.IP \(bu 2
A leading
.B .
is tolerated
.RB ( .quota " \(== " quota ),
so
.BR jq (1)
muscle memory works. A lone
.B .
selects the whole parsed value (useful with
.BR \-\-json ).
.IP \(bu 2
.B Bracket / wildcard syntax is not supported.
.B links[].id
is a parse error \(em use
.B links.0.id ", " links.1.id ", \&..."
or switch to
.BR \-\-json .
.RE
.PP
.B Syntax
.RS
.nf
\-\-field PATH           (short: \-f, alias: \-\-select)
PATH := segment ("." segment)*
segment := <key>         (object lookup)
         | <integer>     (array index, 0\-based)
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Message shapes the selector understands
.RS
.IP 1. 3
.B Real JSON
emitted by the daemon, e.g.
.BR "{\(dqquota\(dq: 10737418240, \&...}" .
.IP 2. 3
.B Legacy flat key/value
lines such as
.BR "userinfo: quota=10737418240, premium=false, email=\(dqa@b.c\(dq, cryptosetup=None" .
These are produced by several `Debug`\-derived runtime responses; the
selector parses them on a best\-effort basis so legacy surfaces can still
be projected. Quotes are honoured, `Some(x)` unwraps to `x`, `None`
becomes `null`.
.IP 3. 3
.B Plain text
(anything else). Only the empty selector `\fB.\fR' matches a plain\-text
response; any other selector exits 2 Usage.
.RE
.PP
.B Bare\-field whitelist
.br
For a small, audited whitelist of read\-only commands the
.B \-\-field
flag may be omitted entirely: trailing bare tokens that do not start with
.B \-
are collected as implicit selectors and rendered in the order you typed
them. Every command on the list has been confirmed (in
.I pcloud\-cli::main::command_accepts_bare_fields
) to take
.I no
variable\-length positional arguments, so there is no ambiguity with
paths / ids / share codes. Any command not on the list must use the
explicit
.B \-\-field
flag.
.PP
.TS
box, tab(|);
l l.
Command|Typical selectors
_
userinfo|email, quota, usedquota, premium, cryptosetup
status|registered, mounted, synced_bytes
health|ok, lag_ms, issues
doctor|checks, summary, exit_code
list\-links|count, links.0.code, links.0.id
list\-upload\-links|count, links.0.code
list\-notifications|count, notifications.0.text
session\-status|active, username
sync\-list|count, syncs.0.localpath, syncs.0.remotepath
integrity\-status|ok, last_run, findings
fs\-status|mounted, mountpoint, cache_bytes
crypto\-status|registered, setup, unlocked
snapshot\-verify|ok, sha3_256, zstd_level, encrypted, size_bytes
.TE
.RS
Source of truth:
.I RUST\-DEV/crates/pcloud\-cli/src/main.rs
function
.IR command_accepts_bare_fields .
All other commands (e.g.
.BR create\-file\-link ", " sync\-add ", " delete\-link )
require the
.B \-\-field
flag so their own positional arguments stay unambiguous.
.RE
.PP
.B Output forms
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B Plain text
(default): one value per line in selector order. Strings are unquoted,
numbers and booleans are printed verbatim, `null` renders as an empty
line, arrays/objects fall back to compact JSON so nothing is lost. Exit
code
.B 0
on success.
.IP \(bu 2
.B With \-\-json :
the response envelope becomes a
.B Filtered
envelope carrying a
.B fields
object that maps each selector path to its selected value, plus the
usual
.BR command ", " status ", and " exit_code
keys for deterministic scripting.
.IP \(bu 2
.B With \-\-quiet :
suppresses the human preamble only; selector output is still written to
stdout. Combine with
.B \-\-field
to produce CI\-safe, one\-value\-per\-line output with no decorations.
.RE
.PP
.B Worked examples (beginner \(-> operations)
.RS
.PP
1. Single plain value (beginner).
.nf
$ pcloudc userinfo email
alice@example.com
.fi
.PP
2. Multiple selectors, plain (stable order).
.nf
$ pcloudc userinfo quota usedquota premium
10737418240
3221225472
false
.fi
.PP
3. Explicit
.BR \-\-field ,
interchangeable with bare positionals on the whitelist.
.nf
$ pcloudc userinfo \-\-field quota \-\-field usedquota
10737418240
3221225472
.fi
.PP
4. Envelope form for CI consumers.
.nf
$ pcloudc \-\-json userinfo quota premium
{"command":"userinfo","status":"ok","exit_code":0,
 "fields":{"quota":10737418240,"premium":false}}
.fi
.PP
5. Array indexing by ordinal.
.nf
$ pcloudc list\-links \-\-field "links.0.code"
XyZ7aBcD
.fi
.PP
6. Compose a loop over a list by ordinal (bracket syntax is
.I not
supported \(em index explicitly).
.nf
COUNT=$(pcloudc list\-links \-\-field count)
for i in $(seq 0 $((COUNT \- 1))); do
    pcloudc list\-links \-\-field "links.$i.code"
done
.fi
.PP
7. Nested object projection from flat legacy output.
.nf
$ pcloudc crypto\-status setup unlocked
true
false
.fi
.PP
8. Empty selector `\fB.\fR' on a JSON response (pretty inspect).
.nf
$ pcloudc \-\-json userinfo \-\-field .
{ \&... entire parsed message \&... }
.fi
.PP
9. Using selectors in pipes with
.BR xargs .
.nf
pcloudc list\-upload\-links \-\-field count      # sanity check
pcloudc list\-upload\-links \-\-field "links.0.code" | xargs echo "cleanup:"
.fi
.PP
10. CI token\-health gate (non\-zero exit on any missing field).
.nf
pcloudc \-\-quiet userinfo email || exit 3   # exit_code 3 == Auth
.fi
.PP
11. Daemon\-free dry run of the selector against a saved response.
.nf
# The selector runs inside the CLI, so `\-\-json` output from an
# earlier invocation can be re\-projected by piping it back in:
pcloudc \-\-json userinfo > last.json
# (human inspection only \(em there is no `\-\-input` flag yet.)
.fi
.PP
12. Scripted integrity sweep summary.
.nf
OK=$(pcloudc integrity\-status ok)
[ "$OK" = true ] || pcloudc integrity\-status findings
.fi
.PP
13. Snapshot\-specific selectors (create and verify).
.nf
# Project individual fields from a snapshot\-create response.
pcloudc snapshot create /tmp/a.tar.zst \-\-field sha3_256 \-\-field size_bytes
# Bare form on the whitelisted snapshot\-verify.
pcloudc snapshot verify /tmp/a.tar.zst sha3_256 zstd_level encrypted
# Envelope form for CI.
pcloudc \-\-json snapshot verify /tmp/a.tar.zst
.fi
Only
.B snapshot verify
is bare\-field whitelisted;
.BR "snapshot create" ", "
.BR "snapshot restore" ", and "
.B snapshot prune
take variable\-length positional paths, so their selectors must use the
explicit
.B \-\-field
flag.
.RE
.PP
.B Error semantics
.br
A selector that does not match exits
.B 2 Usage
and prints a machine\-parseable error naming the failing path and the
available siblings at the last successful step, e.g.:
.RS
.nf
$ pcloudc userinfo quotaa
cli: field not found: 'quotaa'. available: email, premium, quota, usedquota
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
A
.B type mismatch
(indexing a scalar, or keying into an array) also exits 2 and reports
the successful prefix plus the shape encountered
.RB ( object ", " array ", " string ", " number ", " bool ", " null ).
The daemon is never retried; the failure is a local CLI concern.
.PP
.B Interaction with \-\-json and \-\-quiet
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \-\-field " + " \-\-json
emits the
.B Filtered
envelope (see above). Scripts should key off the
.B exit_code
field, never parse stderr.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \-\-field " + " \-\-quiet
strips the human preamble but keeps selector values on stdout; ideal for
cron and
.BR systemd.timer (5)
bodies.
.IP \(bu 2
Field selectors are applied
.I after
the daemon has already sanitised the response, so redacted fields
(secrets, tokens) are simply absent from the JSON object the selector
walks \(em there is no way to deliberately project a secret through the
selector layer.
.RE
.\" =====================================================================
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Every recipe below uses
.B pcloudc
primitives only \(em the built\-in field selector takes the place of
.BR jq (1)
throughout. Each entry lists: the use case, the commands, the expected
output shape, and notes/caveats where the behaviour is non\-obvious.
.PP
.B 1. Back up my Documents folder to a cloud root.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync add ~/Documents /Backups/Documents
pcloudc sync\-list count
.fi
.I Expected:
a numeric count that increases by 1.
.I Note:
`sync add' canonicalises the local path and rejects nested roots; two
entries cannot live inside each other.
.RE
.PP
.B 2. Share a folder with a colleague (read\-only).
.RS
.nf
CODE=$(pcloudc \-\-field code create\-folder\-link /Projects/Alpha)
pcloudc publink send "$CODE" \-\-to bob@example.com \\
    \-\-message "Alpha brief, read\-only"
.fi
.I Expected:
`$CODE' holds the 8\-character public\-link code.
.I Caveat:
legacy whitespace in folder paths must be quoted.
.RE
.PP
.B 3. Public link that expires in 30 days and is password\-protected.
.RS
.nf
CODE=$(pcloudc \-\-field code create\-file\-link /Reports/Q1.pdf)
pcloudc change\-link\-expire   "$CODE" "$(date \-d '+30 days' +%F)"
pcloudc change\-link\-password "$CODE" "$PUBLINK_PASSWORD"
.fi
.I Caveat:
`$PUBLINK_PASSWORD' should come from a secret store
.RB ( pass (1),
systemd\-creds, Vault); do not hard\-code it.
.RE
.PP
.B 4. Mount and browse the cloud as a drive (Linux FUSE).
.RS
.nf
pcloudc mount ~/pCloudDrive
pcloudc fs\-status mounted    # -> true
ls ~/pCloudDrive
pcloudc unmount
.fi
.I Pre\-alpha caveat:
the mounted\-drive runtime is not yet at parity with the legacy C client
\(em expect reduced write/flush fidelity. Track
.BR bd-1du.4 .
.RE
.PP
.B 5. Why is sync paused? (triage loop)
.RS
.nf
pcloudc status
pcloudc health ok lag_ms
pcloudc doctor
pcloudc resume
.fi
.I Note:
`health ok' prints `true' or `false'; `lag_ms' is a number. Feed both to
Prometheus via the daemon's metrics endpoint if you have it enabled.
.RE
.PP
.B 6. Rotate the crypto folder password.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc crypto stop
pcloudc login \-\-user me@example.com \-c         # unlock with NEW password
pcloudc crypto\-status setup unlocked             # -> "true" then "true"
.fi
.I Honest gap:
the `change\-crypto\-pass' family from C is not yet mirrored on the Rust
path; rotation goes through the login\-with\-crypto flow.
.RE
.PP
.B 7. Export account / sync / link state for audit.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc \-\-json sync\-list          > sync\-roots.json
pcloudc \-\-json list\-links         > public\-links.json
pcloudc \-\-json list\-upload\-links  > upload\-links.json
pcloudc \-\-json userinfo           > account.json
.fi
.I Expected:
four stable JSON envelopes, each with `command', `status', `exit_code'.
Safe to diff across days.
.RE
.PP
.B 8. Accept an incoming share invitation.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc list\-incoming\-share\-requests
pcloudc accept\-share\-request 12345
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B 9. Create an upload\-only dropbox for a client.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc create\-upload\-link /Intake/Client\-X
pcloudc list\-upload\-links count      # -> 1 (or more)
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B 10. Force a resync when the daemon missed a local change.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync localscan
pcloudc status
.fi
.I Note:
`localscan' rescans every registered sync root synchronously. On trees
with >100k files this can take minutes.
.RE
.PP
.B 11. Log in non\-interactively from a script (secret never on argv).
.RS
.nf
printf '%s' "$PCLOUD_PASSWORD" | \\
    pcloudc login \-\-user "$PCLOUD_USERNAME" \-\-password\-stdin
.fi
.I Security:
`\-\-password\-stdin' keeps the password out of /proc/*/cmdline and shell
history. Do
.I not
pass `\-\-save\-password' unless you have already reviewed the vault
threat model in
.BR pcloud.conf (5).
.RE
.PP
.B 12. Bulk\-revoke every public link.
.RS
.nf
COUNT=$(pcloudc list\-links count)
for i in $(seq 0 $((COUNT \- 1))); do
    CODE=$(pcloudc list\-links \-\-field "links.$i.code")
    pcloudc delete\-link "$CODE"
done
.fi
.I Caveat:
bracket / wildcard syntax is not supported. Enumerate ordinals with
`count' + `seq' as shown.
.RE
.PP
.B 13. Bulk\-cleanup expired upload links via \fRxargs\fB.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc \-\-json list\-upload\-links > /tmp/up.json
# project one code per line, then remove in parallel
pcloudc \-\-json list\-upload\-links \-\-field "links.0.code" \\
    | xargs \-r \-n1 \-P4 pcloudc delete\-upload\-link
.fi
.I Note:
this example deletes the first code only; iterate with
`seq' + `$i' for full sweeps (see recipe 12). `\-P4' caps to four
parallel daemon calls \(em the IPC socket is single\-threaded.
.RE
.PP
.B 14. Check account quota before a big upload.
.RS
.nf
# Built\-in selector, plaintext (one value per line, selector order):
pcloudc userinfo quota usedquota premium

# Same, as a JSON envelope for programmatic callers:
pcloudc \-\-json userinfo quota usedquota premium

# Full raw envelope when you want every field:
pcloudc \-\-json userinfo
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B 15. Generate and install a shell completion script.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc completion bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/pcloudc
pcloudc completion zsh  > "${fpath[1]}/_pcloudc"
pcloudc completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/pcloudc.fish
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B 16. CI token\-health check (fail fast when the session is dead).
.RS
.nf
# Intended for a GitHub Actions / GitLab CI step. Exits 3 (Auth) when
# the daemon has no live session; scripts treat that as "re\-login".
pcloudc \-\-quiet session\-status active \\
    | grep \-q '^true$' \\
    || { echo "pcloudc session dead"; exit 3; }
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B 17. Nightly DR snapshot at archival compression with verify and GFS prune (cron).
.RS
.nf
# /etc/cron.d/pcloud-rs\-backup
# Level 19 = archival (smaller artifacts, slower compress, same SHA3 pipeline).
# Target path is explicit; bare `pcloudc snapshot' would use the default
# artifact dir from pcloud.conf(5).
15 2 * * *  pcloud-rs  ARTIFACT=/var/backups/pcloud-rs/nightly\-$(date \-u +\\%F).tar.zst; \\
            pcloudc snapshot create "$ARTIFACT" \-\-zstd\-level 19 \\
              \-\-gpg\-recipient dr\-team@example.com \\
              \-\-label "nightly\-$(date \-u +\\%F)" && \\
            pcloudc snapshot verify "$ARTIFACT.gpg" && \\
            pcloudc snapshot prune /var/backups/pcloud-rs \\
              \-\-retention\-days 14 \-\-yes
.fi
.I Tip:
wire the cron exit status to your alerting: exit codes 3 (Auth), 4
(Network), 5 (CryptoLocked) are the three you must page on. Drop
.B \-\-gpg\-recipient
to keep the plain
.B .tar.zst
+ sidecar manifest form (SHA3\-256 still protects integrity; GPG protects
confidentiality).
.RE
.PP
.B 18. Restore onto a fresh host from an offsite artifact.
.RS
.nf
scp dr@offsite:/backups/2026\-04\-15.tar.zst.gpg /tmp/
pcloudc snapshot verify  /tmp/2026\-04\-15.tar.zst.gpg
pcloudc snapshot restore /tmp/2026\-04\-15.tar.zst.gpg \-\-yes
pcloudc status
.fi
.I Caveat:
`\-\-yes' is mandatory; restore is destructive and will refuse to run
otherwise. The sidecar
.B .manifest.json
MUST sit next to the archive (same basename + `.manifest.json').
.RE
.PP
.B 19. Integrity sweep as a systemd.timer.
.RS
.nf
# ~/.config/systemd/user/pcloud-rs\-integrity.service
[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/pcloudc integrity\-status ok last_run
# ~/.config/systemd/user/pcloud-rs\-integrity.timer
[Timer]
OnCalendar=daily
Persistent=true
# Enable:   systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now pcloud-rs\-integrity.timer
.fi
.I Note:
`integrity\-status' is bare\-field\-whitelisted, so the unit body has no
.BR jq (1)
dependency. Alert on exit code `\(!=' 0.
.RE
.PP
.B 20. Stop a backup device and reclaim local state.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync\-list count              # before
pcloudc sync remove 17
pcloudc sync\-list count              # after (decreased by 1)
.fi
.RE
.\" =====================================================================
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
Always start with
.B pcloudc doctor
\(em it runs the full check battery (socket, vault, config, daemon PID,
mount health) and prints exactly what to fix. The entries below are
ordered by how often they come up in practice.
.TP
.B 1. "connection refused" / "No such file or directory" (exit 4)
.I Diagnosis:
the daemon is not running or the IPC socket is stale.
.RS
.nf
pgrep \-u "$USER" pcloudd
ls \-l "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pcloud/pcloud-rs/pcloud.sock"
.fi
.I Fix:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc doctor
pcloudc start           # launches pcloudd
pcloudc status
.fi
If the socket exists but the daemon is gone, remove the stale socket
and restart. Inspect the daemon log at
.I $PCLOUD_LOG_PATH
(see
.BR ENVIRONMENT )
or run `pcloudc \-\-debug start' in the foreground for a full trace.
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B 2. "permission denied" on the IPC socket (exit 4)
.I Diagnosis:
the daemon was started by a different user, or the runtime dir lost its
.B 0700
mode (NFS homedirs are notorious for this).
.RS
.nf
stat \-c '%a %U' "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pcloud/pcloud-rs"
# expected: 700  <your\-username>
.fi
.I Fix:
stop the daemon, remove the runtime dir, restart.
.B Never
loosen the mode to 0755 \(em the CLI and daemon will both refuse.
.RE
.TP
.B 3. "2FA timeout" or code never arrived (exit 3)
.RS
.nf
pcloudc send\-tfa\-sms
pcloudc send\-tfa\-notification
pcloudc submit\-tfa 123456
.fi
If SMS is unreliable, log in with
.B \-T code
(authenticator app) or
.B \-T recovery
plus a one\-time recovery code.
.RE
.TP
.B 4. Mount fails or reports "device busy"
.RS
.nf
pcloudc doctor
pcloudc unmount
fusermount \-u ~/pCloudDrive 2>/dev/null || true
PCLOUD_FORCE_UMOUNT=1 pcloudc unmount
pcloudc mount ~/pCloudDrive
.fi
On macOS install macFUSE. On Linux confirm your user is in the
.I fuse
group (or that
.B user_allow_other
is set in
.IR /etc/fuse.conf ).
The mounted\-drive runtime is still pre\-alpha; see
.BR bd-1du.4 .
.RE
.TP
.B 5. Sync stuck or "paused" indefinitely
.RS
.nf
pcloudc doctor
pcloudc health
pcloudc sync localscan
pcloudc resume
.fi
If a specific sync root is wedged, remove and re\-add it.
.RE
.TP
.B 6. "Conflict detected" on a file
The daemon keeps both versions and renames the local copy with a conflict
suffix. Inspect with `pcloudc sync\-list', resolve by hand, then
`pcloudc sync localscan'.
.TP
.B 7. Exit code 5 ("CryptoLocked")
Your command needs the crypto folder unlocked.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc crypto\-status setup unlocked
pcloudc crypto start      # prompts for the crypto password
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B 8. `\-\-field' rejects a name ("field not found", exit 2)
.I Diagnosis:
the selector does not exist in the response shape.
.RS
.nf
$ pcloudc userinfo quotaa
cli: field not found: 'quotaa'. available: email, premium, quota, usedquota
.fi
.I Fix:
pick from the `available' list (which is sorted). See
.B FIELD SELECTION .
.RE
.TP
.B 9. "links[].id" is a parse error (exit 2)
.I Diagnosis:
bracket / wildcard syntax is not supported; only dotted segments and
numeric indices.
.I Fix:
enumerate with
.B count
+ a shell loop over ordinals. See
.B EXAMPLES
recipe 12.
.TP
.B 10. Login succeeds but nothing persists across restarts
.I Diagnosis:
durable auth\-token persistence is opt\-in. By default the vault is not
written.
.I Fix:
set
.B PCLOUD_DURABLE_AUTH_TOKENS=1
or flip
.B features.durable_auth_tokens_enabled = true
in
.BR pcloud.conf (5).
.B Security:
read the vault threat model first; the vault is
.B 0600
and owner\-checked.
.TP
.B 11. Trace id appears in stderr but nothing reaches my collector
.I Diagnosis:
the CLI only originates `traceparent'; the OTLP export happens
daemon\-side and requires (a) a build with the
.B tracing-otlp
feature, and (b)
.B [observability.tracing]
enabled in
.BR pcloud.conf (5).
See
.B OBSERVABILITY / DISTRIBUTED TRACING .
.TP
.B 12. Metrics port refuses connections
.I Diagnosis:
by default the metrics endpoint binds to
.BR 127.0.0.1 .
.I Fix:
set
.B PCLOUD_METRICS_BIND_ALL=1
.I only
if the interface is firewalled; do not expose Prometheus scrape
endpoints to the internet.
.TP
.B 13. "vault file has wrong mode or owner" at startup
.I Diagnosis:
a previous run left the auth\-token vault with group/other bits, or the
file is owned by a different user (common after a
.BR sudo (8)
invocation).
.I Fix:
delete the vault and log in again \(em do
.I not
silently
.BR chmod (1)
it back into place, that masks a real intrusion signal.
.RS
.nf
rm \-f "$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pcloud/pcloud-rs/auth_token"
pcloudc login \-\-user me@example.com
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B 14. `TRACEPARENT' is set but the CLI appears to ignore it
.I Diagnosis:
the envvar is malformed; the CLI drops it silently at the boundary
(documented honest behaviour).
.I Fix:
verify the shape is
`\fB00\-<trace:32hex>\-<span:16hex>\-<flags:2hex>\fR', or pass
`\-\-trace\-id <HEX>' explicitly and let the CLI synthesize a valid
parent.
.TP
.B 15. "duplicate sync root" / "nested sync root" (exit 7)
.I Diagnosis:
either the local path is already registered, or it lives inside another
registered root (nested sync is rejected on purpose).
.I Fix:
.RS
.nf
pcloudc sync\-list count                          # how many roots
pcloudc sync\-list \-\-field "syncs.0.localpath"
pcloudc sync remove <id>                          # to adjust
.fi
.RE
.\" =====================================================================
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PCLOUD_ROOT
Override the runtime state directory (config, vault, sockets, logs).
.TP
.B PCLOUD_CONFIG
Path to the configuration file; same as
.BR \-\-config .
.TP
.B PCLOUD_ENV
Active profile name; selects an entry in
.BR pcloud.conf (5).
.TP
.B PCLOUD_API_MODE
Transport mode for the API profile.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_API_HOST
Override the API hostname.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_API_PORT
Override the API port.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_API_SERVER_NAME
Override the TLS SNI / server name.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_API_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
Connect timeout in milliseconds.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_API_READ_TIMEOUT_MS
Read timeout in milliseconds.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_PLUGINS_ENABLED
Enable the plugin runtime (default off).
.TP
.B PCLOUD_PLUGIN_ALLOW_NETWORK
Allow plugins to make network calls (default off).
.TP
.B PCLOUD_PLUGIN_ALLOW_SYNC_CONTROL
Allow plugins to drive sync state (default off).
.TP
.B PCLOUD_PLUGIN_ALLOW_CRYPTO
Allow plugins to see crypto events (default off).
.TP
.B PCLOUD_DURABLE_AUTH_TOKENS
Opt into persisting the auth token in the vault (default off).
.TP
.B PCLOUD_CACHE_SIZE_GB
Mount cache size in GiB; set via
.BR \-\-cache-size .
.TP
.B PCLOUD_DEFAULT_MOUNTPOINT
Default mountpoint when
.B mount
is invoked without an argument.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_LOG_PATH
Daemon log file path.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_FS_EVENT_LOG
Filesystem event log path.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_LOG_LEVEL
Daemon log level (e.g.
.BR info ", " debug ", " trace ).
.TP
.B PCLOUD_FUSE_OPTS
Extra comma\-separated FUSE options forwarded to the mount.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_FORCE_UMOUNT
When set to
.BR 1 ", " true ", " yes " or " on
(case\-insensitive), a bare
.B pcloudc unmount
is promoted to a forced unmount for the currently configured
mountpoint. See the
.B unmount
subcommand for path\-resolution order.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_METRICS_PORT
Bind port for the metrics exporter.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_METRICS_BIND_ALL
When
.BR 1 ,
bind the metrics exporter on all interfaces (otherwise localhost).
.TP
.B PCLOUD_AUDIT_HMAC_KEY
HMAC key material for the local audit log. See
.BR SECURITY .
.TP
.B PCLOUD_MIGRATE_LEGACY_PATHS
When set, consult legacy C\-client paths during first run.
.TP
.B PCLOUD_CHAOS
Test\-only fault injection; leave unset in production.
.TP
.B TRACEPARENT
W3C Trace Context parent to adopt for this CLI invocation. When set to
a well\-formed
.B 00\-<trace:32hex>\-<span:16hex>\-<flags:2hex>
value, the CLI uses it verbatim as the
.B traceparent
on the outgoing
.B RequestEnvelope
to the daemon. Malformed values are dropped silently. See the
.B OBSERVABILITY / DISTRIBUTED TRACING
section.
.PP
Full syntax and security notes for every variable live in
.BR pcloud.conf (5).
.\" =====================================================================
.SH FILES
.PP
The tables below enumerate every path
.B pcloudc
and
.BR pcloudd (1)
touch on disk, the default location on each platform, the Unix mode the
daemon enforces, and the owner. Defaults are derived from
.I RUST\-DEV/crates/pcloud\-config/src/paths.rs
(path roots) and
.I RUST\-DEV/crates/pcloud\-config/src/runtime.rs
(mode enforcement). All four directory modes default to
.B 0o700
and the daemon refuses to boot if any group/other bit is set on a
production profile.
.PP
.B Linux (XDG Base Directory).
In the table below these prefixes stand in for the real XDG roots so the
rows fit on an 80\-column terminal:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B $CFG
= $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pcloud/pcloud-rs
.IP \(bu 2
.B $DAT
= $XDG_DATA_HOME/pcloud/pcloud-rs
.IP \(bu 2
.B $CACHE
= $XDG_CACHE_HOME/pcloud/pcloud-rs
.IP \(bu 2
.B $RT
= $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pcloud/pcloud-rs
.RE
.TS
box, tab(|);
l l l l.
Purpose|Default path|Mode|Owner
_
Config dir|$CFG/|0700|user
Config file|$CFG/pcloud.conf|0600|user
Auth\-token vault|$CFG/auth_token|0600|user
Data dir (state)|$DAT/|0700|user
SQLite store|$DAT/pcloud.db|0600|user
Journal dir (writeback)|$DAT/journal/|0700|user
Upload progress sidecars|$DAT/staging/ino\-<N>.upload\-progress|0600|user
Download .part files|<dest>.part (adjacent)|0600|user
Cache dir|$CACHE/|0700|user
FUSE staging / thumbs|$CACHE/|0700|user
Runtime dir|$RT/|0700|user
IPC socket|$RT/pcloud.sock|0600|user
Daemon log|$PCLOUD_LOG_PATH (unset: $DAT fallback)|0600|user
Filesystem event log|$PCLOUD_FS_EVENT_LOG (unset: $DAT fallback)|0600|user
.TE
.PP
Fallbacks when
.BR XDG_* _HOME
is unset: config -> ~/.config, data -> ~/.local/share, cache -> ~/.cache,
runtime -> no invented default (the daemon refuses to create a runtime
directory outside a tmpfs).
.PP
.B macOS.
.TS
box, tab(|);
l l l l.
Purpose|Default path|Mode|Owner
_
Config / data|~/Library/Application Support/com.pcloud.pcloud-rs/|0700|user
Vault file|<above>/auth_token|0600|user
SQLite store|<above>/pcloud.db|0600|user
Cache|~/Library/Caches/com.pcloud.pcloud-rs/|0700|user
Runtime (no XDG analogue)|<cache>/pcloud-rs\-runtime/|0700|user
IPC socket|<runtime>/pcloud.sock|0600|user
.TE
.PP
.B Windows.
Mode columns are advisory: ownership is enforced via ACLs (the vault
loader refuses files not owned by the current SID). Let
.B %APP%
= %APPDATA%\epcloud\epcloud-rs and
.B %LOC%
= %LOCALAPPDATA%\epcloud\epcloud-rs for the paths below.
.TS
box, tab(|);
l l l.
Purpose|Default path|Notes
_
Config|%APP%\econfig\e|SID\-restricted ACL
Data|%APP%\edata\e|SID\-restricted ACL
Cache|%LOC%\ecache\e|SID\-restricted ACL
Runtime|%LOC%\ecache\epcloud-rs\-runtime\e|SID\-restricted ACL
IPC socket|<runtime>\epcloud.sock|owner\-only AF_UNIX
.TE
.PP
.B Overrides.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B PCLOUD_ROOT
overrides the state root (config + data + cache + runtime colocated).
.IP \(bu 2
.B PCLOUD_CONFIG
overrides only the config\-file path; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.IP \(bu 2
Full override matrix and validation rules live in
.BR pcloud.conf (5).
.RE
.\" =====================================================================
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0 Ok
Command completed successfully. Not retryable (there is nothing to retry).
.TP
.B 1 GenericError
Unclassified failure. Retryable only after investigation.
.TP
.B 2 Usage
Argument or flag was wrong. Not retryable without fixing the command line.
.TP
.B 3 Auth
Bad credentials, expired session, or 2FA cancelled. Not retryable without
re\-authenticating; run
.BR "pcloudc login" .
.TP
.B 4 Network
Transport, IPC, or network failure. Retryable with backoff.
.TP
.B 5 CryptoLocked
A required crypto folder is locked. Not retryable until you run
.BR "pcloudc crypto start" .
.TP
.B 6 Unavailable
Daemon is down or the feature is disabled on this build. Retryable after
.BR "pcloudc start" .
.TP
.B 7 Conflict
Conflicting state (for example, duplicate sync root). Not retryable until
the conflict is resolved.
.TP
.B 8 Internal
Daemon reported an internal error. Not safely retryable; capture
.B pcloudc doctor
output and file a bug.
.\" =====================================================================
.SH PLATFORM NOTES
.TP
.B Linux
FUSE mounts require the
.I fuse
kernel module and, for shared mounts, the
.I allow_other
option. XDG directories are used by default.
.TP
.B macOS
FUSE mounts require macFUSE. State lives under
.IR ~/Library .
.TP
.B Windows
Mounted drives require WinFsp. Paths are stored under
.BR %APPDATA% " and " %LOCALAPPDATA% ;
ownership checks on the vault use ACLs instead of POSIX mode bits.
.\" =====================================================================
.SH PARTIAL TRANSFER RESUME
.PP
.B Uploads.
The daemon maintains a crash\-safe per\-inode progress sidecar
.RI ( ino\- <inode> .upload\-progress )
under the staging directory for every in\-flight upload. Each sidecar is
written via write\-temp + fsync + rename + fsync(dir) and carries the
server\-assigned upload id, the acknowledged byte offset, a SHA\-256 of the
acknowledged prefix, and a heartbeat timestamp. On daemon restart the
bootstrap path, and on mount (re)activation the mount runtime, replay every
sidecar by calling the
.B upload_status
protocol method and classifying the result as one of seven outcomes
.RB ( Resumed ", " ServerAhead ", " SidecarTrimmed ", " Expired ", "
.BR Stalled ", " Unparseable ", " BackendError ).
Uploads idle beyond the 10\-minute heartbeat timeout with no server\-side
progress are surfaced as
.B Stalled
and aborted with a retryable error. Killing
.B pcloudd
mid\-upload is safe; the next start resumes from the last acknowledged
offset.
.PP
.B Downloads.
The resumable HTTP download path is a library API
.RB ( fetch_download_resumable
in
.BR pcloud\-proto )
that writes to
.IR <dest>.part ,
reissues interrupted fetches with
.BR "Range: bytes=N\-" ,
re\-hashes the on\-disk prefix against the expected final hash before trusting
it (O(file\-size) cost, documented), deletes the
.I .part
file on SHA\-256 mismatch, and falls back to a full replace when the server
answers with
.B 200 OK
without advertising
.BR "Accept\-Ranges: bytes" .
Not every daemon\-side download call site has been migrated to this entry
point yet:
.B fetch_download_verified
callers still restart interrupted downloads from byte 0. See
.I docs/book/src/operations/partial\-transfers.md
for the per\-call\-site migration status.
.\" =====================================================================
.SH OBSERVABILITY / DISTRIBUTED TRACING
.PP
When the daemon is built with the
.B tracing\-otlp
Cargo feature and
.B [observability.tracing]
is enabled in
.BR pcloud.conf (5),
.B pcloudc
participates in W3C Trace Context propagation so a single CLI
invocation renders as one end\-to\-end trace across
CLI \(-> daemon \(-> pCloud API in whichever OTLP backend the operator
wires up (Jaeger, Grafana Tempo, Datadog agent, Honeycomb, New Relic,
generic OTel collector). The feature is off by default and has zero
runtime cost when the daemon is built without it.
.PP
.B End\-to\-end flow.
.RS
.nf
pcloudc \-\-trace\-id <HEX> status
       |
       |  (1) globals.rs synthesises a traceparent
       v
RequestEnvelope { request, traceparent }
       |
       |  (2) IPC over pcloud.sock
       v
pcloudd dispatch.rs
       |
       |  (3) try_from_wire pulls out traceparent
       |     (bare Request frames are still accepted)
       |  (4) opens pcloudd.dispatch parent span
       |  (5) opens per\-backend child span
       v
backend (status, shares, transfer, \&...)
       |
       |  (6) tokio tasks that outcall pCloud API
       |     inherit the child span context
       v
OTLP exporter \(-> collector
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Traceparent source (precedence).
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-trace\-id <HEX>
global flag. Accepts a 32\-character lowercase\-hex W3C trace id. The CLI
synthesises a fresh random 16\-byte span id and wraps them in a
canonical parent of the form
.BI 00\- <trace-id> \- <span-id> \-01
(sampled=1, explicitly forced). Use this when a support ticket carries a
pre\-assigned correlation id.
.IP \(bu 2
.B TRACEPARENT
environment variable. When neither
.B \-\-trace\-id
is given nor the value is empty, the CLI adopts a well\-formed
.B TRACEPARENT
verbatim as the parent context. Malformed values are dropped
.I silently
at the boundary (documented honest behaviour); the CLI then behaves as
if the variable were unset.
.IP \(bu 2
Neither set: the CLI leaves
.B traceparent = None
on the wire. The daemon still serves the request normally; it simply
has no parent context to attach spans to, so its own spans become
roots. This is intentional \(em the CLI does
.I not
auto\-sample or invent trace ids just because the feature is compiled in.
.RE
.PP
.B Wire shape.
The CLI transports the context to the daemon inside
.B pcloud_ipc::RequestEnvelope
which is `\fB{ request, traceparent }\fR'. Untraced callers omit the
field entirely. Byte compatibility with pre\-envelope peers is preserved
by
.B RequestEnvelope::try_from_wire
which accepts a bare
.B Request
frame and treats it as
.BR "RequestEnvelope { request, traceparent: None } ."
.PP
.B Daemon span hierarchy.
On the daemon side
.IR pcloud\-daemon/src/dispatch.rs
stages the CLI\-supplied traceparent into a thread\-local immediately
before calling
.BR dispatch .
The dispatcher reads (and clears) it, opens a
.B pcloudd.dispatch
parent span with attributes
.RB ( method ", " exit_code ", " status ),
and each backend (status, shares, transfer, public\-link, crypto, sync)
opens its own child span beneath it.
.PP
.B Echoed id.
Every traced invocation prints a single line to stderr before the
command result so the operator can paste it into a ticket:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[trace: 00\-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\-00f067aa0ba902b7\-01]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Worked example: correlate a \fRpcloudc status\fB through a
downstream OTel collector.
.RS
.nf
# Operator side: generate a trace id and fire the CLI.
TID=$(openssl rand \-hex 16)
pcloudc \-\-trace\-id "$TID" status
# ... pcloudc emits to stderr:
#   [trace: 00\-<TID>\-<span>\-01]

# Downstream: query your OTel backend for this trace id and
# expect at minimum these spans (in parent \-> child order):
#   pcloudd.dispatch          (method=status)
#       status_backend.query  (child)
#           api.status        (grpc/http outcall)
.fi
.RE
Attach the operator\-visible trace id to the support ticket; the OTel
backend will light up the complete ladder.
.PP
.B Collector endpoint.
The OTLP/HTTP endpoint and optional auth headers are configured under
.B [observability.tracing]
in
.BR pcloud.conf (5)
and owned by the daemon process. The CLI never contacts the collector
directly; it only originates the
.B traceparent
carried inside the envelope.
.PP
.B Honest caveats (pre\-alpha).
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The end\-to\-end CLI \(-> daemon \(-> backend span ladder has been verified
against a
.I local
OTLP sink only. No live interop with a production\-grade OTLP backend
(Honeycomb, Datadog, Tempo) has been certified in this release.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B Pkcs11Hsm
code path and the mounted\-drive
.BR FUSE
code path are
.I not
yet instrumented; operations that traverse those paths will show gaps
in the child span tree.
.IP \(bu 2
The CLI does not auto\-sample when `\-\-trace\-id'/
.B TRACEPARENT
are both absent. If you want every invocation traced, set
.B TRACEPARENT
from a wrapper script that generates a fresh parent each time.
.IP \(bu 2
Sampling policy is
.I daemon\-side .
The flags byte on the CLI\-synthesised traceparent is always
.B 01
("sampled=1, explicitly forced"); per\-feature head\-sample gates belong
in the daemon's
.B [observability.tracing]
block.
.RE
.\" =====================================================================
.SH SECURITY
The auth token vault and IPC socket are owner\-only and rejected on load if
the mode or owner is wrong; two\-factor authentication, when enabled on the
account, is enforced at login time; the crypto folder is protected by a
local\-key\-derived password that the daemon never persists to disk in the
clear. For full details of file modes, HMAC keys, and transport policy see
.BR pcloud.conf (5).
